The proposed residual transform operator, $\LL=R^1_{n,\zeta}$, yields more accurate solutions than the standard third-order local differencing operator, $\LL=T^1_n$. While not displayed here, this superior performance is consistent in cases with significant blurring, as demonstrated in  \cref{fig:blur_test}.
Since the variability within the smooth regions of an underlying function is often unknown a priori, similar to the denoising problem, applying an unsuitable regularizer can lead to significant errors in the deblurring scenario. This is evident in the left and the middle columns for both cases of $\gamma=0.01$ and $\gamma=0.05$, where using the first order local differencing operator ($\LL = T_n^0$) results in considerable error and a pronounced ``staircasing'' artifact. By contrast, the residual transform operator {\em of the same order} successfully mitigates the ``staircasing'' effect by accurately capturing the local variability within smooth regions. The bottom row displays the spatial errors for the corresponding approximations in the top row. Notably, the error plot for $p=1$(right) confirms that the solution ${\bm x}_R$ continues to outperform ${\bm x}_{loc}$. 


