During motor learning, humans enhance environmental perception through multiple strategies. For one thing, they exploit environmental cues—a concept aligned with Gibson’s affordance theory in ecological psychology—which posits that behavior is guided by action possibilities offered by objects~\cite{gibson1966senses,bach2014affordance}. Second, internal perceptual encoding capabilities are refined. For example, contextual sensory representations shaped by prior experience mitigate skill forgetting~\cite{forano2021direct,ferrari2022updating}, and multisensory integration ensures robust manipulation~\cite{camponogara2021integration}. Moreover, external information sources, such as augmented sensory feedback or prompt cues, accelerate the learning processes~\cite{taylor2014explicit}.

Drawing on these insights from human motor learning, VLA model post-training techniques for enhancing environmental perception are summarized by: Sec.~\ref{Sec:Affordance-Guided Learning} affordance-guided learning, Sec.~\ref{Sec:EEM} enhanced encoder for manipulation, and Sec.~\ref{Sec:ERM} enhanced representation for manipulation.

\subsubsection{Affordance-Guided Learning}

\label{Sec:Affordance-Guided Learning}

The concept of affordance is introduced in psychology by Gibson in the mid-20th century, describing the actionable possibilities offered by environment~\cite{gibson1966senses}. Before the emergence of VLA models, affordance has already been extensively explored in robotic manipulation~\cite{yamanobe2017brief}. In this context, affordance denotes the interaction between agent capabilities and environmental features that gives rise to behavior~\cite{stoffregen2018affordances, chemero2018outline}. Because affordance captures object–environment relationships critical for manipulation, affordance-guided methods are presented in Sec.~\ref{Sec:Enhancing Environmental Perception}.

Affordances in robotic manipulation can be divided into two categories: manipulation‐related and spatial. Manipulation‐related affordances encode strategies for robot–object interaction, specifying how to manipulate objects~\cite{li2024improving}. Spatial affordances convey positional information, indicating where manipulation should occur~\cite{lee2024affordance}. Recent studies embed affordance knowledge within VLA models to enhance manipulation performance~\cite{li2024improving, huanga3vlm, lee2024affordance, yuanrobopoint, pan2025omnimanip, nasirianyrt, xu2025a0}.

Although some studies exploit only spatial affordances to generate guidance signals for robotic manipulations~\cite{lee2024affordance}, most approaches leverage both manipulation-related and spatial affordances inherent to objects~\cite{huanga3vlm, yuanrobopoint, pan2025omnimanip, nasirianyrt, xu2025a0}. A common implementation encodes affordance information directly within language instructions, enabling explicit affordance transfer via enriched prompts~\cite{pan2025omnimanip, yuanrobopoint, huanga3vlm}. Alternatively, pre-trained VLM can be fine-tuned on dedicated affordance datasets, supplying affordance representations for action outputs~\cite{ji2025robobrain, xu2025a0, nasirianyrt, mu2024robocodex}. Furthermore, affordance knowledge may be integrated into a chain-of-thought framework to structure reasoning from spatial perception to manipulation~\cite{wei2022chain, li2024improving}.

Table~\ref{table:Environment} summarizes representative affordance-based methods for improving environmental perception in VLA models. The observed performance improvements underscore the importance of incorporating object-affordance priors to enhance manipulation robustness.


\subsubsection{Enhanced Encoder for Manipulation}

\label{Sec:EEM}

Refining environmental perception through encoder optimization is an intuitive strategy for enhancing VLA model manipulation performance~\cite{gupta2024pre, wen2025data}. For instance, simple supervised fine-tuning with a fully unfrozen vision encoder has been shown to increase manipulation success rates~\cite{zhang2024effective}. This section examines strategies for augmenting the encoder’s perception abilities.

Knowledge distillation enhances perception capabilities in VLA models~\cite{roy2024m2distill, shang2024theia, liang2024never}. By transferring multimodal representations from a high capacity teacher network, distillation mitigates catastrophic forgetting during supervised fine-tuning—wherein new task-specific updates can overwrite existing features~\cite{roy2024m2distill}. Meanwhile, distillation can also transfer specialized skills—such as 3D visual understanding, from high-performance models to the base encoder~\cite{liang2024never, shang2024theia}. These complementary advantages preserve and augment encoder perception, improving manipulation performance.


As VLA models remain inherently a type of multimodal model, visual question answering~\cite{kim2025visual}—a widely adopted approach for enhancing multimodal understanding—can be employed to improve the encoders of VLA models~\cite{song2024robospatial, zhou2025chatvla}. VQA integration yields enhancements in specific perceptual capabilities, such as 3D perception~\cite{song2024robospatial}, as well as the overall multimodal environmental awareness~\cite{zhou2025chatvla}. 

Encoder performance can also be elevated through architectural refinements. Such flexible techniques include lightweight architectures ~\cite{wen2025tinyvla, zhang2025mole}, task-oriented modular designs~\cite{marza2024task}, and incorporating additional information~\cite{zhaovlas}. These refinements enhance computational efficiency or expand the representational capacity of the perception module.

Table~\ref{table:Environment} summarizes key algorithms for enhancing environmental perception in VLA encoders. The resulting improvements in task success rate and computational efficiency demonstrate that stronger environmental perception directly enhances manipulation performance.

\subsubsection{Enhanced Representation for Manipulation}

\label{Sec:ERM}

Enhanced task-related environmental representations simplify the decision-making process during the policy head’s action inference. Unlike the methods in Sec.~\ref{Sec:EEM}, the approaches discussed here primarily employ external modules—such as enriched language prompts~\cite{zhu2025objectvla, niullarva} and representation selection or alignment mechanisms~\cite{huang2025otter, lan2025bfa}—rather than solely relying on vision encoder fine-tuning~\cite{gupta2024pre, wen2025data}.

A direct solution is to use more informative prompts guiding the vision-language encoder to generate task-relevant representations for manipulation~\cite{di2024keypoint, niullarva, zhu2025objectvla, qu2025spatialvla, zhong2025dexgraspvla, chen2024vision}, analogous to prompt engineering in large language models~\cite{sahoo2024systematic}. These studies usually leverage powerful external models, such as SAM~\cite{kirillov2023segment} and DINO-X~\cite{ren2024dino}, to explicitly extract information from the environment. These features are then incorporated into prompt fed into VLA models. An interesting conclusion is that prompts may also be represented as abstract tokens rather than natural language~\cite{di2024keypoint}. In summary, the prompts are generally crafted based on more informative environmental information, enhancing the original robotic model in perception.

Another strategy involves post-processing of extracted vision–language representations~\cite{huang2025otter, lan2025bfa, xu2025vla}. For example, Huang et al.~\cite{huang2025otter} propose an alignment module that synchronizes visual embedding with instruction-derived representations to emphasize task-relevant features. Lan et al.~\cite{lan2025bfa} propose a fusion module that scores representations from multiple inputs and aggregates them according to these scores. Besides these approaches, the representation can also be enhanced through adding a new modality. Zhang et al.~\cite{zhang2025vtla} incorporate tactile inputs into the baseline VLA model, demonstrating improved contact-rich dexterous manipulation capabilities.


Table~\ref{table:Environment} summarizes primary techniques for enriching environmental representations in manipulation tasks. Observed performance gains indicate that integrating informative environmental cues substantially enhances robotic manipulation. 


\subsubsection{Summary}

Enhanced environmental understanding is a direct strategy for improving VLA model manipulation (Table~\ref{table:Environment}). Affordance-guided learning (Sec.~\ref{Sec:Affordance-Guided Learning}) employs object-centric priors to interpret target-object properties for manipulation. Encoder enhancement (Sec.~\ref{Sec:EEM}) and representation refinement (Sec.~\ref{Sec:ERM}) augment VLA models' environmental perception to boost overall performance. The advantages of these techniques can be summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Enhancing environmental perception can leverage established successes in fields like CV and multimodal learning. Lessons from prior studies—along with pre-trained backbones and public datasets—can contribute to robotic environmental understanding.
    \item Integrating high-performance yet efficient perception modules can obviously improve VLA models' performance, as environmental perception is essential for generalization and robustness in VLA model manipulation.
\end{itemize}

While enhanced environmental perception yields substantial benefits for VLA models, it incurs additional computational and procedural overhead. For example, affordance-guided learning requires extra steps to extract object-affordance information, and encoder or representation refinement demands supplementary training or architectural modifications. Despite these costs, the resulting improvements in manipulation performance justify environmental perception augmentation as a valuable avenue for VLA post-training.

