Embodiment awareness in humans is supported by the internal model framework, which comprises forward and inverse models~\cite{de2008neural,imamizu2000human}. During motor learning, forward model updates predict future limb states to enable smooth, stable movements, while the inverse model transforms desired state changes into appropriate motor commands~\cite{flanagan2003prediction}. Both models contribute to enhanced body awareness and sensorimotor integration. Further enhancements arise from specialized neural controllers that interface directly with the action space. For instance, multiple internal-model modules within the cerebellum support coordinated control of diverse motor tasks~\cite{imamizu2000human}, and basal ganglia circuits assemble discrete motor primitives into fluid action sequences~\cite{graybiel1998basal}.

Based on these lessons from human motor learning, the summary of VLA model post-training techniques for improving embodiment awareness are summarized by: Sec.~\ref{Sec:Forward kinematics learning} forward/inverse kinematics learning and Sec.~\ref{Sec. Action Head Designing} action head designing.

\subsubsection{Forward/Inverse Kinematics Learning}

\label{Sec:Forward kinematics learning}

Kinematics for robotic systems encompasses both forward and inverse processes. Forward kinematics predicts the resulting pose given a specified action, while inverse kinematics computes the action required to reach a target pose. Because kinematic relationships differ across robot platforms, enabling a VLA model to learn these relationships is crucial for modeling the target embodiment.

In forward kinematics learning, the VLA model forecasts future proprioceptive information—such as joint angles and end-effector positions and trajectories—from current observations and task instructions~\cite{zhang2024effective, li2025hamster, li2024mastering, niullarva}. Predicting these signals enables implicit acquisition of the embodiment’s forward mapping. This is typically implemented via an auxiliary prediction head that maps fused representations to proprioceptive outputs~\cite{li2025hamster}. Inverse kinematics learning infers actions that cause transitions between successive sensory observations~\cite{jin2024learning, niullarva}. By predicting embodiment-specific actions from sensory inputs and instructions, the model learns the inverse action generation mapping of the given embodiment.

Table~\ref{table:Embodiment} summarizes key methods and their performance outcomes. Results indicate that incorporating both forward and inverse kinematics prediction modules enhances manipulation stability and success rates. Moreover, extending longer-horizon action prediction can further enhance embodiment awareness.

 \subsubsection{Action Head Designing}

\label{Sec. Action Head Designing}

The action head in VLA models transforms vision–language representations into manipulation commands, translating task understanding and environmental perception into motor actions. Consequently, action-head design is critical to enhance embodiment awareness.

One class of the action head designs employs adapters to reparameterize the multimodal encoder for action decoding~\cite{szot2024grounding, zhu2025efficient}. Szot et al.~\cite{szot2024grounding} introduce semantically aligned adapters for discrete actions and a learnable module for continuous actions, optimizing representations for decoding. Zhu et al.~\cite{zhu2025efficient} develop lightweight adapters that enable rapid task adaptation across diverse manipulation scenarios.

Another design addresses the architecture and output format of the action head. Except for investing the scaling of action head~\cite{liubidirectional}, the architectures remain relatively underexplored. Output format innovations include action chunking and tokenization schemes. Action chunking allows VLA models to generate a sequence of actions to increase the control frequency~\cite{liubidirectional, song2025accelerating, kim2025fine}. However naive implementations may incur instability and high computational cost. To mitigate these issues, bidirectional decoding stabilizes chunked-action generation~\cite{liubidirectional}, while parallel decoding mechanisms accelerate execution~\cite{song2025accelerating, kim2025fine}. Moreover, frequency-based tokenization schemes replace simple regression or discrete tokens, enhancing decoding efficiency~\cite{pertsch2025fast}.

Other studies integrate multiple action heads within VLA systems rather than redesigning a single head~\cite{pan2024vision, liu2025hybridvla, wang2024scaling, zheng2025universal}. This architecture leverages complementary policies to mitigate individual limitations~\cite{pan2024vision, liu2025hybridvla}. For example, Liu et al.~\cite{liu2025hybridvla} present a unified framework that combines diffusion-based and auto-regressive policies, preserving the pre-trained strengths of the auto-regressive head while maintaining action continuity. Architectures assign embodiment-specific heads to different robot platforms, asserting that a common latent representation supports distinct decoding pathways for cross-embodiment generalization~\cite{wang2024scaling, zheng2025universal}.

Table~\ref{table:Embodiment} summarizes key action-head design methods and their outcomes. The results demonstrate that carefully designed action heads enhance VLA model performance in both success rate and efficiency.

\subsubsection{Summary}

Embedding forward and inverse kinematics, as well as action‐space representations, enhances embodiment awareness in VLA models. Beyond improvements in success rate, two key advantages emerge:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Most techniques are plug-and-play, requiring no changes to other components of VLA models. Forward or inverse kinematics learning adds complexity only during post-training and imposes no inference overhead, since the policy head alone can generate actions directly. Similarly, action-head redesign affects only the output module, leaving vision–language representation learning unchanged.
    \item Data for enhancing embodiment awareness are easy to collect. In addition to standard action–state demonstration datasets, trajectories from random policies can be used for kinematics learning, simplifying data collection and experimental setup.
\end{itemize}

Although each mechanism independently boosts performance, they can be combined with environmental perception and task comprehension strategies. Combined integration approaches are discussed in Sec.~\ref{Sec:Mutiple Component Integration}.


