The development of task understanding is a critical component of human motor learning. Humans acquire this understanding through instructional guidance and expert demonstrations, which enhance safety and accelerate skill acquisition~\cite{csibra2011natural}. Leveraging advanced cognitive and perceptual capabilities, they demonstrate generalized behaviors to novel contexts~\cite{merel2019hierarchical}. Moreover, humans can decompose tasks into simpler subtasks, reducing long-horizon planning complexity and improving learning efficiency~\cite{ramkumar2016chunking}. This hierarchical approach mirrors the organization of the human nervous system, where high-level circuits plan actions and low-level circuits execute motor commands~\cite{merel2019hierarchical}.

Based on these findings, the strategies of VLA model post-training for deepening task comprehension are summarized as follows: Sec.~\ref{Sec:HRI} human robot interaction and Sec.~\ref{Sec:HTM} hierarchical task manipulation.

\subsubsection{Human-Robot Interaction}
\label{Sec:HRI}
Current manipulation tasks for VLA models remain naive for human experts. Transferring expert task knowledge via interactive mechanisms therefore offers a promising enhancement. Such knowledge transfer can be achieved through explicit or implicit processes.

Explicit methods integrate human experts directly into the manipulation loop, using interaction data to post-train VLA models and improve efficiency, success rate, and safety~\cite{chen2025conrft, xiang2025vla, wu2025robocopilot}. A characteristic of explicit interaction is the direct collaboration between experts and the VLA model. For instance, VLA-Expert~\cite{xiang2025vla} integrates a small fraction of expert actions into a pre-trained VLA model during the manipulation tasks to boost performance. These demonstrations, even with only sparse human actions, can improve the VLA model's performance. ConRFT~\cite{chen2025conrft} combines expert guidance with online RL to enforce safe and correct actions.

Implicit methods encode expert knowledge by aligning human preferences or embedding expertise within demonstration data~\cite{rafailov2023direct, chen2025fdpp, tian2024maximizing, huang2025adversarial}. This alignment parallels knowledge distillation~\cite{gou2021knowledge}, treating human experts as teacher models. Preference alignment techniques adjust the policy head to match expert preferences~\cite{peng2024improving, chen2025fdpp}, while representation alignment maps human attention patterns during manipulation to VLA model representations~\cite{tianmatters, tian2024maximizing}. Although some preference-alignment studies target autonomous driving or diffusion policies, their post-training policy-head strategies are directly applicable to VLA models. Demonstration-based alignment, as in Huang et al.~\cite{huang2025adversarial}, uses expert interventions to generate corner-case trajectories with high prior density, yielding improved sample efficiency.

Table~\ref{table:Task} summarizes the representative techniques described above. These methods highlight the considerable gains afforded by expert priors. Given that VLA models remain distant from expert-level task understanding, systematic integration of human knowledge is essential for advancing manipulation capabilities.



\subsubsection{Hierarchical Task Manipulation}

\label{Sec:HTM}

Hierarchical task decomposition draws on human problem‐solving strategies, whereby complex, long‐horizon objectives are partitioned into manageable sub‐goals~\cite{kahneman2011thinking, voytek2015oscillatory}. Similarly, VLA models can decompose intricate manipulation tasks into simpler primitives to improve learning efficiency and task performance~\cite{yuan2024pre, kang2024clip, hu2023look, myerspolicy, liu2025robodexvlm, ji2025robobrain, smith2024steer, di2024diffusion, zhang2024grape, zheng2025dataplatter, lin2025onetwovla}.


Effective decomposition requires robust task understanding, which may be provided by multimodal foundation models~\cite{zhang2024grape, hu2023look} or human experts~\cite{zheng2025dataplatter, smith2024steer}. For instance, Zheng et al.~\cite{zheng2025dataplatter} partition tasks with high spatial‐reasoning demands into a spatial‐analysis phase and a physical‐interaction phase, enabling targeted data collection that boosts performance. However, human‐driven splits can be subjective, motivating automated decomposition via pretrained VLMs~\cite{yuan2024pre, kang2024clip, hu2023look, myerspolicy, liu2025robodexvlm, ji2025robobrain, di2024diffusion, zhang2024grape, lin2025onetwovla}.


A direct strategy applies each decomposed sub-goal as task-informed guidance for manipulation~\cite{ji2025robobrain, smith2024steer, di2024diffusion}. These methods exploit a VLM’s inherent task-understanding capabilities to segment manipulation tasks—via language instructions and visual observations—into sequential sub-goals, implicitly transferring task priors to the robot policy. The efficacy of decomposition can be further enhanced through the online adaptation~\cite{yuan2024pre} or closed-loop control architectures~\cite{kang2024clip, radford2021learning, liu2025robodexvlm, lin2025onetwovla}, which optimize planning of sub-goal sequencing to improve performance.

The decomposition of tasks into sub-tasks also optimizes VLA models' training process~\cite{myerspolicy, zhang2024grape, zheng2025dataplatter}. For one thing, sub-goals are inherently more learnable and enable the model to focus on tasks where model performance remains sub-optimal, thereby improving training efficiency and manipulation outcomes~\cite{myerspolicy, zheng2025dataplatter}. Moreover, sub-goals create supplementary supervised signals for model training~\cite{zhang2024grape}, permitting reward assignment to imperfect yet informative manipulation trials.

Table~\ref{table:Task} summarizes representative hierarchical decomposition techniques and their performance outcomes. Results indicate that structured task decomposition significantly increases success rates, reflecting effective transfer of expert task knowledge to VLA models via sub‐goal guidance.

\subsubsection{Summary}

Deepening task comprehension in VLA models often leverages domain experts—either human demonstrators or multimodal foundation models. The primary advantages of this approach include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Experts possess deeper insights into manipulation than base VLA models, enabling decomposition of complex tasks into manageable sub-goals and offering more effective guidance for learning.
    \item In safety-critical scenarios, expert-informed task understanding can improve operational safety metrics that VLA models are difficult to learn by themselves.
\end{itemize}

Given these benefits, achieving deeper task comprehension typically requires expert guidance—whether human or large models—which increases post-training overhead for VLA models. Consequently, developing more efficient mechanisms for embedding expert knowledge remains imperative.


