Beyond isolated enhancement of individual components, human motor learning often entails concurrent adaptation across multiple perspectives. For example, motor skills are acquired via trial-and-error processes reinforced by both reward-driven and error-driven feedback systems~\cite{kasdin2025natural, truong2023error}. Moreover, internal forward models construct a comprehensive world model which, together with inverse models, predicts the outcomes of actions within environmental contexts~\cite{diester2024internal}. Finally, effective skill acquisition benefits from structured training schedules and exposure to diverse, informative experiences~\cite{czyz2024high}.

Based on these insights, efforts of VLA model post-training for deepening task comprehension are structured into following perspectives: Sec.~\ref{Sec. RL} reinforcement learning, Sec.~\ref{Sec. VIP} visual interaction prediction, and Sec.~\ref{Sec. EFT} active dataset processing.


\subsubsection{Reinforcement Learning}

\label{Sec. RL}

RL has been widely adopted in robotic research~\cite{kober2013reinforcement}. More recently, RL frameworks—particularly reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF)—have demonstrated efficacy in foundation model~\cite{kaufmann2023survey}. These advances have inspired the application of RL in VLA models. In RL, an agent iteratively interacts with its environment under a task-specific reward function, enabling policy optimization. This process is believed to effectively learn policy from suboptimal demonstration and maintain generalization~\cite{liu2025can, mark2024policy}. Accordingly, RL inherently encompasses the three fundamental components of the VLA model learning: environments, embodiments, and tasks.

Instability in RL-based VLA model post-training is a major challenge~\cite{peng2024improving, hu2024flare, stachowiczlifelong, springenberg2024offline, guo2025improving, chen2025conrft, nakamotosteering, mark2024policy}. This instability is mainly caused by two factors: (1) transitioning from the pre-training stage to RL-based post-training, (2) Q-function overestimation~\cite{hu2024flare, stachowiczlifelong}. To mitigate these issues, behavioral cloning can be incorporated during post-training, either via an auxiliary behavioral-cloning loss~\cite{hu2024flare, chen2025conrft, springenberg2024offline} or via an iterative training process between supervised learning and RL~\cite{guo2025improving}. Some studies try to stabilize the model by freezing the vision language encoder~\cite{chen2025conrft,guo2025improving}. Proven RL-stabilization techniques—such as clipped double-Q learning~\cite{fujimoto2018addressing}, ensemble critics~\cite{chenrandomized}, and critic normalization~\cite{ball2023efficient}—can also contribute to VLA model post-training; Stachowicz et al.~\cite{stachowiczlifelong} successfully integrate these methods into a lifelong-learning framework. Moreover, the Q-function can also be derived by offline RL for online re-arranging the actions~\cite{mark2024policy, nakamotosteering}. The online manipulation data can be further applied for improving policy and the Q-function~\cite{mark2024policy}. Additionally, some studies also demonstrate human preference signals can be leveraged to improve performance, training efficiency, and safety~\cite{chen2025conrft, hu2024flare}. 

Foundation model priors can also enhance RL-based post-training for VLA models. In particular, foundation models generate reward signals that eliminate the need for handcrafted reward engineering and provide dense feedback in complex environments~\cite{nam2023lift, patel2025real, lee2024affordance, zhai2023building}. Reward signals can be derived directly from a foundation model based on language instructions and visual observations~\cite{nam2023lift, lee2024affordance, zhai2023building}, or formulated via programmatic reward functions~\cite{patel2025real}. Beyond rewards designing, the foundation model can also improve few-shot learning abilities in offline RL~\cite{shiunleashing}.

Table~\ref{table:Muti} summarizes representative RL-based post-training methods for VLA models. In addition to significant gains in sample efficiency and success rates, RL approaches support iterative interaction and continuous adaptation, enabling VLA models to refine their manipulation policies over time.

\subsubsection{Visual Interaction Prediction}

\label{Sec. VIP}

Visual interaction prediction involves forecasting the effects of action sequences on the embodiments and environments. This capability requires generating action sequences, simulating the resulting embodiment behavior, and predicting subsequent environmental changes. Its effectiveness was first demonstrated during pre-training on unlabeled demonstration data~\cite{wu2023unleashing, li2025gr, cheang2024gr}, and it has since become a widely adopted post-training strategy for VLA models~\cite{zhao2024vlmpc, chen2025vision, zhou2024minedreamer, hu2024video, zhang2025up, zeng2024learning, li2024virt, hao2024language}. These techniques relate closely to the kinematics learning in Sec.~\ref{Sec:Forward kinematics learning}. However, those methods primarily address how actions affect the embodiment, without accounting for environmental and task-specific influences.

One intuitive approach involves forecasting future observation frames using action-conditioned predictive models~\cite{zhao2024vlmpc, chen2025vision, zhou2024minedreamer}. Such frame prediction can be embedded within a model-predictive-control (MPC) framework for forward-kinematics learning, wherein control objectives are optimized by minimizing pixel-wise differences between predicted and target frames~\cite{zhao2024vlmpc, chen2025vision}. Predictive models also facilitate inverse‐kinematics learning: Zhou et al.~\cite{zhou2024minedreamer} generate future frames from current observations and instruction embeddings, then use both predicted and actual frames to infer action commands. Alternatively, forecasting may occur in a latent feature space by predicting future observation representations~\cite{hu2024video, zhang2025up}. Latent‐space prediction reduces the complexity of pixel‐level synthesis and enables robust dynamics modeling even when frame predictions are imperfect.

Beyond frame forecasting, interaction prediction can be formulated via various strategies~\cite{zeng2024learning, li2024virt, jin2024learning, hao2024language}. Zeng et al.~\cite{zeng2024learning} predict intermediate interaction frames between action initiation points and subsequently apply a detection module to localize interaction regions. Li et al.~\cite{li2024virt} generate action guidance by masking target objects and constructing point-flow representations, rather than synthesizing full-frame predictions. Although these approaches remain preliminary relative to direct frame forecasting, they offer valuable insights for advancing interaction prediction.




Table~\ref{table:Muti} summarizes methods for visual interaction prediction. These studies demonstrate significant improvements in both success rate and data efficiency. Although integrating visual interaction prediction increases VLA model complexity, it contributes to substantial performance gains.


\subsubsection{Active Dataset Processing}

\label{Sec. EFT}

High-quality manipulation datasets for VLA models are expensive to construct, motivating the use of minimal yet informative demonstration sets for post-training. Achieving this efficiency requires active demonstration selection and augmentation strategies that leverage comprehensive knowledge of manipulation tasks, environmental contexts, and robotic embodiments.

An intuitive approach to active processing is selecting informative demonstrations for post-training, as demonstration quality—not quantity—determines VLA policy gains. Lin et al.~\cite{lin2024data} empirically demonstrate that diversity in environments and objects is more critical than sheer demonstration count, highlighting the value of varied demonstrations. Accordingly, informativeness‐based selection and weighting have been proposed: Bagatella et al.~\cite{bagatella2024active} use information‐gain estimation to curate fine‐tuning datasets, while Wu et al.~\cite{wu2024swbt} select high‐similarity, imperfect trials to augment training, enhancing data efficiency. Demonstration importance also guides data collection: Zheng et al.~\cite{zheng2025dataplatter} prioritize spatial‐control scenarios, and Gao et al.~\cite{gao2024efficient} decompose tasks by object properties to reduce sample requirements via data combination designs.

Another active processing strategy augments limited high‐cost demonstrations with low‐cost, automatically collected or generated samples~\cite{zhouautonomous, xue2025demogen, wang2024robogen, jin2024learning}. Self‐improvement methods leverage foundation‐model guidance to enable autonomous demonstration gathering, reducing dependence on expensive expert data~\cite{zhouautonomous, wang2024robogen}. Generative frameworks either synthesize manipulation trajectories directly~\cite{jin2024learning} or apply trajectory‐editing techniques for demonstration augmentation~\cite{xue2025demogen}.

Active processing during post‐training can further enhance data efficiency through curriculum design, augmentation, and auxiliary tasks~\cite{wen2025dexvla, goyal2024rvt, wang2025robobert}. Examples include decomposing long‐horizon tasks into shorter sub‐tasks via curriculum learning to reduce training burden~\cite{wen2025dexvla}, applying data augmentation to diversify training inputs~\cite{wang2025robobert}, and incorporating auxiliary training tasks to enrich the learning objectives~\cite{goyal2024rvt}.


Table~\ref{table:Muti} presents representative active dataset processing techniques for improving data efficiency in VLA model post‐training. These approaches either achieve comparable manipulation performance with substantially fewer high-cost demonstrations or leverage lower-cost data sources to reduce overall training expense.


\subsubsection{Summary}

Simultaneously enhancing VLA models across multiple dimensions constitutes a holistic approach. Unlike single‐focus methods, multi‐component strategies integrate improvements in environment, embodiment, and task modules to achieve synergistic gains. The benefits of these strategies include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Addressing multiple improvement components rather than a single feature can yield substantial overall performance gains.
    \item Multi‐component methods can be combined with targeted techniques to reinforce specific capabilities.
\end{itemize}

These methods offer a comprehensive solution when the primary performance bottleneck is unknown. However, they introduce additional architectural and training complexity; if a single module is identified as the limiting factor, focused optimization remains a preferable strategy.




\section{Challenges and Future Trends}

\subsection{Data and Evaluation}

\subsubsection{Fairness Evaluation}

Although benchmarks and simulation environments exist for evaluating VLA models~\cite{yu2020meta, liu2023libero, li2024evaluating, mees2022calvin}, fair cross-study comparisons remain challenging. Variations in experimental setups—including hyperparameters, task definitions, and hardware configurations—complicate result comparison. Additional variability, such as random initialization seeds and initial robot states, also affect outcomes. For example, Zhang et al.~\cite{zhang2024effective} observe success rate fluctuations exceeding 30\% under the same conditions. Whereas simulators can control seeds and initial states, real-world evaluations lack this level for reproducing. Therefore, conducting a sufficient number of validation trials to achieve statistical significance is essential; however, determining the optimal numbers of repetition remains an open problem~\cite{andrychowicz2021matters}.

Given the variability in experimental settings, the results summarized in Tables~\ref{table:Environment}, \ref{table:Embodiment}, \ref{table:Task}, and \ref{table:Muti} are drawn directly from the original studies. Consequently, identifying a universally superior technique for any specific objective is infeasible. Instead, each algorithm can only be evaluated based on performance gains against the baselines and configurations employed in its respective study.


The preceding discussion underscores the need for a unified evaluation benchmark in robotic learning, analogous to ImageNet in CV~\cite{deng2009imagenet}. The emergence of ImageNet catalyzes numerous algorithmic innovations and research directions within CV. Establishing a comparable benchmark for manipulation is more challenging—owing to the high cost of demonstrations and the diversity of operational environments, but lessons drawn from ImageNet suggest that a widely adopted standard could accelerate progress in the VLA model community. Moreover, fairness evaluation is also a critical concern in both human learning and behavior analysis. Metrics and experimental protocols from human learning studies can be adapted to assess fairness and performance in VLA model evaluations.

\subsubsection{Data Efficiency}




Robot manipulation data is costly; effective utilization and acquisition are therefore essential. Data efficiency can be enhanced through post-training approaches and optimized data-collection strategies. Nonetheless, identifying effective methods for leveraging demonstration data remains an open challenge.


Post‐training strategies encompass both algorithmic innovations and data augmentation. Algorithmic approaches—such as incorporating auxiliary tasks—have demonstrated potential for improving data efficiency~\cite{goyal2024rvt}; however, these methods lack comprehensive empirical evaluation and are primarily tailored to enhance downstream performance rather than optimize data efficiency. In contrast, data augmentation has received limited attention in the VLA domain despite its recognized importance~\cite{wang2025robobert,xue2025demogen}. Data augmentation used for VLA models typically draw spon techniques developed for enhancing perception, underscoring the need for manipulation specific augmentation strategies.


Effective data collection focuses on two objectives: minimizing the number of demonstrations required for a given task and identifying a minimal set of tasks that maximize the acquisition of manipulation skills. For the first objective, importance score metrics have been proposed to rank demonstrations~\cite{bagatella2024active}; however, these metrics offer limited guidance on sampling procedures. Alternatively, increasing the proportion of spatial reasoning demonstrations has been shown to benefit VLA model post‐training~\cite{zheng2025dataplatter}, but concrete guidelines for dataset construction remain undeveloped. Regarding the second objective, preliminary studies indicate that demonstration coverage can be reduced without compromising skill acquisition~\cite{gao2024efficient}; nevertheless, these findings are confined to controlled laboratory settings and require validation in open‐world environments.

Although data-efficiency improvements have been pursued in VLA post-training, current VLA models still underperform humans significantly. This performance gap motivates the incorporation of efficient learning mechanisms inspired by human motor learning into VLA post-training. Moreover, studies of human learning demonstrate that structured curriculum design and selective data sampling can markedly enhance learning efficiency. These observations indicate that VLA model post-training can also emphasize curriculum scheduling and data collection strategies to optimize training processes.


\subsection{Manipulation Ability}

\subsubsection{Incorporating Additional Modalities}

Although vision and language constitute the core modalities for VLA models, additional modalities such as tactile sensing play a critical role in manipulation tasks. Integrating tactile feedback into VLA architectures represents a promising research direction. Recent efforts have introduced vision–tactile–language–action (VTLA) models~\cite{zhang2025vtla} and augmented baseline VLA models with force-feedback signals~\cite{yu2025forcevla}. These methods demonstrate particular effectiveness in contact-rich scenarios but remain in early development owing to challenges in tactile data acquisition and sensor fusion.

Current VLA architectures often fail to capture complete scene understanding. Some models can process multi-view images~\cite{team2024octo}, yet still lack direct depth perception. Addressing this limitation requires incorporating additional modalities—such as depth sensing—alongside raw visual inputs.

Similar to human motor learning, integrating multi-modal perception into VLA models offers a promising path to enhance manipulation capabilities. Insights from human manipulation emphasize strategies for fusing multiple sensory modalities and evaluating each modality’s contribution to task performance, which can guide the design and selection of sensors to improve VLA model performance. Beyond algorithmic improvements, realizing this potential requires concurrent advances in sensing hardware and the development of high-fidelity simulation environments.


\subsubsection{Continual/Lifelong Learning}

Several studies have shown that the vision–language encoder should be fully unfrozen during post-training~\cite{wang2025robobert, zhang2024effective}, indicating that perception abilities of current VLA models are still limited. However, while full-parameter fine-tuning enhances task-specific performance, it induces catastrophic forgetting by degrading previously acquired vision–language representations and manipulation skills, both of which are essential for generalization.

Addressing these challenges requires continual learning frameworks for post-training VLA models, enabling acquisition of new skills without forgetting prior ones. Preliminary investigations have demonstrated the potential of continual learning in VLA training~\cite{liang2024never, roy2024m2distill}. However, these approaches have been evaluated predominantly on small-scale manipulation tasks and often resemble multi-task learning rather than true lifelong learning. 

The forgetting phenomenon is also common in human learning. Incorporating mechanisms inspired by human memory consolidation into a continual learning framework for VLA models—which enables incremental acquisition of diverse manipulation skills and progression toward generalist capabilities—represents a promising avenue for future research.



\subsubsection{Open-World Deployment and Generalization}  

Current evaluations of VLA models are confined to laboratory settings or specialized simulators, where SOTA methods have demonstrated high performance and certain generalization~\cite{kimopenvla, brohan2023rt, intelligence2025pi_, intelligence2025pi05}. Although these proof-of-concept studies are encouraging, they remain insufficient for broader, real-world deployment. In open-world environments, various uncertainties—such as changes in lighting and occlusions from obstacles—arise during perception, posing additional environmental robustness challenges for VLA models.

Real-world environmental conditions are highly dynamic. While certain variations can be reproduced in controlled or simulated settings, open-world fluctuations are far less predictable. Consequently, VLA models are required to reliably complete designated tasks across a diverse range of scenarios. To date, post-training research has focused on task-level performance within fixed environments; extending these efforts to encompass environmental generalization in open-world contexts is equally critical. Humans, by contrast, exhibit relatively strong adaptability to diverse environments. Extracting and integrating the mechanisms underlying human environmental generalization could significantly enhance VLA model robustness.



\subsection{Safety and Ethics}

\subsubsection{Explainable and Trust in VLA Models}

Explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) is a rapidly growing area in intelligent systems research~\cite{dwivedi2023explainable}. Although XAI remains mostly unexplored in VLA models, its importance cannot be ignored. First, improved system interpretability facilitates the identification of model shortcomings, supporting targeted performance enhancements. Second, by explicitly understanding VLA model shortcomings, developers can implement targeted improvements prior to deployment to bolster robotic system performance.

Moreover, enhancing explainability helps cultivate user trust in VLA models. Unlike foundation models in NLP and CV, robotic foundation models are required to interact with the physical world, where errors can cause direct harm. Even prior to VLA-era systems, in certain scenarios—despite robots achieving or exceeding expert-level performance—users still preferred human operators~\cite{kundu2021ai}. Embedding XAI mechanisms within VLA architectures is therefore a critical prerequisite for safe and reliable real-world deployment.

To enhance the interpretability of VLA models, methods from explainable AI (XAI) research can be adapted and extended to VLA architectures. Alternatively, mechanisms of human learning can inform the design of inherently interpretable models or be used to elucidate observed model behaviors.


\subsubsection{Safety in VLA Models}

Safety is a critical concern in robotic manipulation, as interactions between robots and the physical environment can introduce danger. However, current research on post-training VLA models places limited concentration on safety. Some approaches ensure safety through the human-in-the-loop framework~\cite{chen2025conrft}. While this guided training can guarantee safety during the learning phase, it only encodes safety preferences into the VLA model and does not guarantee safety during autonomous manipulations. Moreover, human-in-the-loop training is costly. Consequently, advancing inherent safety mechanisms for VLA models remains an urgent challenge. Future work should investigate cost-effective training schemes with integrated safety guarantees and embed safety requirements as explicit model constraints to ensure reliable operation across diverse manipulation scenarios.

\section{Conclusion}

This survey presents post-training techniques for VLA models in robotic manipulation through the lens of human motor learning, organized around three core components: environments, embodiments, and tasks. A high-level taxonomy, mirroring the way of human skill acquisition, categorizes research efforts aimed at enhancing model capabilities across these components, with representative methods summarized within each category. Challenges and limitations of current VLA post-training approaches are then identified. By aligning VLA post-training with human learning processes, this review offers a novel perspective for understanding and improving model adaptation. Additionally, it provides a practical reference for researchers targeting specific aspects of VLA models and highlights emerging trends to guide future investigations.


