\begin{abstract}
Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated promise in reasoning tasks and general decision-making in static environments. In long-term planning tasks, however, errors tend to accumulate, often leading to unsafe or inefficient behavior, limiting their use in general-purpose settings. We propose a modular actor-critic architecture in which an LLM actor is guided by LTLCrit, a trajectory-level LLM critic that communicates via linear temporal logic (LTL). Our setup combines the reasoning strengths of language models with the guarantees of formal logic. The actor selects high-level actions from natural language observations, while the critic analyzes full trajectories and proposes new LTL constraints that shield the actor from future unsafe or inefficient behavior. The architecture supports both fixed, hand-specified safety constraints and adaptive, learned soft constraints that promote long-term efficiency. Our architecture is model-agnostic: any LLM-based planner can serve as the actor, and LTLCrit serves as a logic-generating wrapper. We formalize planning as graph traversal under symbolic constraints, allowing LTLCrit to analyze failed or suboptimal trajectories and generate new temporal logic rules that improve future behavior. We evaluate our system on the Minecraft diamond-mining benchmark, achieving 100\% completion rates and improving efficiency compared to baseline LLM planners. Our results suggest that enabling LLMs to supervise each other through logic is a powerful and flexible paradigm for safe, generalizable decision making.
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}



Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently demonstrated strong performance on diverse reasoning and decision-making tasks, from natural language understanding, reasoning~\citep{JH-KCC:22, JW-XW-DS-MB-FX-EC-QVL-DZ:22}, and code generation~\citep{YL-DC-JC-ADL:22, MC-JT-HJ-GB:21}. However, much of this success has been in static, text-based settings. To deploy AI systems in real-world scenarios, it is essential to build agents capable of safe and efficient decision-making in complex, partially observable environments with long-term objectives and uncertainty.

Embodied environments such as  Minecraft~\citep{LF-GW-YJ-AM-AA:22}, Habitat~\citep{MS-AK-OM-JM:19}, Alfworld~\citep{MS-XY-MAC-YB-AT-MH:20} serve as useful testbeds for the next generation of agents. These environments require agents to perceive complex states, plan for long-horizon problems, under sparse supervision and rewards. Recent work has begun to explore the use of LLMs in embodied settings. SayCan~\cite{MA-AB-NB-YC-OC-BD-CF-CF-KG-KH:22} uses LLMs to decide affordance filtered actions for short-horizon robotic tasks, while Voyager~\citep{GW-YX-YJ-AM-CX-YZ-LF-AA:23} leverages an LLM to autonomously explore and obtain as many new materials as possible in Minecraft.  

Despite these advances, LLMs continue to struggle with long-term sequential planning in embodied dynamical environments. While LLMs can often generate coherent short-term plans or respond to individual prompts, they lack the consistency, memory, and iterative refinement required for solving multi-step tasks where intermediate actions must align with long-term objectives. These challenges are especially pronounced in open-ended domains like robotics or Minecraft, where the agent must choose from a large set of possible actions while reasoning about tools, environment dynamics, and partial observability.

Recent evaluations~\citep{SK-KV-LG-MV-KS-SB-LS-ABM:24} show that LLMs struggle to reliably solve classical planning problems without the aid of external verification. Even simple domains expose brittleness: small prompt variations can cause cascading errors, and plans often violate task preconditions or logical constraints. These limitations have spurred the development of hybrid frameworks that pair LLMs with verifiers~\citep{TS-VH-RSS-NK-TLP-LPK:22, XG-DK-US-LQ-HZ-GD-PS-BH:24}. While these methods improve correctness, they rely on hand-written task models. 

Ultimately, a central challenge is enabling foundational models like LLMs to act as reliable, autonomous agents in real-time, safety-critical, multi-agent environments such as healthcare~\citep{AH-CP-JQ-LHS-HJWLA:18}, automated transportation~\citep{YW-RJ-SSZ-CL-CH-ZW-ZY-QZ:23}, and domestic assistance~\citep{RB-NMR-EHN-JB-MZ:25}. In each of these contexts, both safety and efficiency are crucial. Unsafe or inconsistent behavior can lead to failures in physical systems, and inefficient or incompetent agents rapidly lose the trust of human collaborators~\citep{CE-LPR:23}. We argue that planning architectures should combine the reasoning strengths of LLMs with the formal structure of symbolic logic to ensure both flexibility and safety in real-world decision making.


\subsection{Related works}

\paragraph{Temporal Logic for Planning and Reinforcement learning}
Linear Temporal Logic (LTL)~\citep{AP:77} is a formal language for specifying temporal properties of systems, for example, conditions that must hold at the next timestep given the current state, or conditions that must always hold, or eventually become true. LTL has been widely used in robot motion planning~\citep{GEF-HKG-GJP:05}, for safe planning and control~\citep{TW-UT-RMM:12}, and even in reinforcement learning~\cite{MA-RB-RE-BK-SN-UT:18}. In each of these applications, LTL is used to specify safety constraints; however, one may also use it to specify efficiency constraints, as will be discussed in our graph formulation. 

\paragraph{Language Models for Planning and policy learning}
Recent work has shown some promise in terms of LLMs' abilities to perform in short-horizon planning tasks~\citep{WH-PA-DP-IM:22}. Particularly,~\cite{MA-AB-NB-YC-OC-BD-CF-CF-KG-KH:22} utilizes an LLM to asses the value of a given action, and weights it with an affordance function which quantifies the feasibility or success rate of the action given the current state of the system. It achieves a strong success rate in short-horizon mobile manipulation tasks. ~\cite{WH-FX-TX:22} introduces a closed-loop inner monologue by feeding language-based feedback back into the LLM during execution, enabling re-planning in dynamic environments. Simultaneously, other works such as ~\cite{SK-KV-LG-MV-KS-SB-LS-ABM:24} suggest that LLMs do not show reliability while operating autonomously, and require external critics. Our work builds directly upon these ideas, augmenting LLMs with formal verifiers. Finally, a majority of the work in LLM planning and policy learning talks about the success rate. Our emphasis is not only on completion rates, but also on speed of completion.

\paragraph{Integrating Symbolic Reasoning with LLMs}
There is also a growing body of work at the intersection of LLMs and temporal logic; however, the key emphasis is on using LLMs as tools for translation between natural language and LTL constraints~\cite{YC-RG-YZ-CF:23, JXL-ST-AS:23}. Another line of work uses these translators in the loop~\citep{ZY-SSR-AS-ST:24}, enforcing these constraints to filter out unsafe actions during execution. Compared to prior literature, which enables the use of static handcrafted rules, we allow an LLM critic to come up with further LTL constraints that show empirical efficiency gains in LLM planning problems.

\paragraph{LLM-guided Actor-Critic Architectures}
Recent work explores hybrid actor-critic setups where LLMs guide planning and evaluation, often using natural language feedback or prompt optimization loops~\citep{YD-KL-XJ-ZJ-GL:23, RY-FY-JL-SY-YZ-ZT-XL-DY:25}. These models rely on soft, unstructured feedback and lack formal guarantees, particularly in multi-step embodied reasoning tasks. Our LTL-based trajectory critic offers interpretable, symbolic evaluation aligned with both safety and efficiency constraints.


\subsection{Contributions}

We propose LTLCrit,  a novel Linear Temporal Logic-based Critic, which augments and boosts the performance of existing off-the-shelf LLM planners by protecting the planners against unsafe and inefficient actions. The composition of LTLCrit with an existing LLM planner leads to a novel symbolic actor-critic architecture designed to solve long-horizon planning tasks in dynamic, embodied environments using large language models (LLMs). Planning is decomposed across two timescales, a online actor loop that proposes high-level actions based on current state descriptions, and a offline critic loop that imposes symbolic constraints learned from past trajectories. Our modular decomposition aligns with LLMs’ strengths in local reasoning while addressing their weaknesses in long-term consistency over sequential domains.

LTLCrit is domain-agnostic, extensible to a variety of embodied tasks, and naturally integrates with existing LLM-based planners. It is the first to use symbolic temporal logic as a communication protocol between actor and critic modules, enabling interpretable, verifiable, and generalizable decision making. Our contributions are threefold.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item 
    \textbf{Symbolic Actor-Critic Architecture}: We propose a two-timescale architecture where an LLM actor generates high-level actions in real time, and an LLM critic periodically analyzes trajectories to induce Linear Temporal Logic (LTL) constraints over symbolic abstractions of state and action. These constraints are used to prune unsafe or inefficient decisions, leading to improved sample efficiency and task performance. Our formulation allows constraints to be automatically discovered and continuously refined over time.
    \item \textbf{Communication via Temporal Logic}: We introduce a novel mechanism for actor-critic interaction based on symbolic temporal logic. In contrast to traditional reinforcement learning critics or natural language feedback, our critic outputs verifiable, machine-checkable LTL constraints. LTL constraints provide strong safety guarantees, enforces logical consistency, and enables constraint reuse across similar states and tasks. We also present a graph-theoretic abstraction of planning under temporal logic, which highlights the role of constraint pruning in reducing planning complexity.

    \item \textbf{Empirical Validation in Embodied Settings:} We evaluate our method on challenging long-horizon tasks in Minecraft, where agents must autonomously mine a diamond. Our critic can improve both the consistency of behavior and average performance for two popular LLM planners, SayCan~\citep{MA-AB-NB-YC-OC-BD-CF-CF-KG-KH:22} and InnerMonologue~\citep{WH-FX-TX:22}.
\end{enumerate}

Together, these contributions advance the goal of safe, efficient, and interpretable LLM-based decision-making in complex, dynamic environments. Our symbolic actor-critic framework bridges the gap between natural language reasoning and formal planning, opening up new possibilities for trustworthy embodied agents.



