\section{Experiments}
\subsection{Experimental Setup}
Next, we evaluate our architecture on a long-horizon diamond mining task in Minecraft. We utilize OpenAI's GPT 4.1 for the LLM actor, and o3-mini for the LLM critic. Temperatures are set to 0.1 to reduce stochasticity in code generation. Our environment is implemented via Mineflayer's JavaScript API, which exposes structured access to agent state and motor control primitives. On top of this, we define a set of atomic action wrappers (for example, \texttt{mineBlock}, \texttt{craftItem}) and expose structured observations to the LLMs. Further details are discussed in Appendix~\ref{appendix:minecraft-interface}.

\subsection{Baselines}
Our modular architecture allows us to utilize off-the-shelf LLM actors and improve them using our symbolic critic. In particular, we focus on augmenting two existing LLM-based planners that represent distinct paradigms in LLM control.

\paragraph{SayCan\citep{MA-AB-NB-YC-OC-BD-CF-CF-KG-KH:22}} The SayCan actor consists of two stages: affordance filtering and value-guided selection. First, an affordance function decides if a high-level action is feasible given the current agent and environment state. Then, a language model assigns each filtered feasible high-level action a value estimate reflecting how helpful it is for achieving the final goal (in our case, mining a diamond). The agent then executes the feasible action with the highest predicted value. In order to implement an affordance filter in our framework, we construct the filter using a series of LTL laws. The list of these laws that form the affordance filter is in Appendix~\ref{appendix:laws}.

\paragraph{InnerMonologue\citep{WH-FX-TX:22}}
Inner Monologue augments an LLM planner with a persistent stepwise reasoning across timesteps. At each step, the agent outputs a natural language ``thought" along with a code snippet to execute, and then receives feedback from the environment. The loop is repeated, with the prompt at each round including the previous thoughts, code, and outcome. This enables implicit reflection and planning over time. 

We augment both of these planners with our critic, allowing us to measure how symbolic constraints improve performance and safety. To derive the LTL constraints, we analyze the actor-only trajectories using the LLM critic, which generates candidate LTL rules that promote safe and efficient behavior. The resulting rules are added to a growing constraint set. If suboptimal behavior is observed under the current constraint set, we re-run the resulting trajectories through the critic, which may propose additional constraints or refinements. This iterative process continues until performance stabilizes. In practice, InnerMonologue required 2 iterations to stabilize. We present all relevant prompts and discovered LTL laws in the appendix.



We do not compare to vision-based Minecraft agents trained on pixel observations with continuous motor outputs~\citep{KN-PA-AS-YC-HH-SS-RF:23, SC-ZW-XM-AL-YL:23, ZW-SC-GC-AL-XM-YL:23}. Our goal is to improve LLM planning in environments with structured state and action spaces. Our framework assumes access to state descriptors and motor-level wrappers, which are common in many robotics pipelines. As such, our results are aligned with improving task efficiency and safety in high-level LLM-driven control. 



