\begin{abstract}
Today, social media platforms are significant sources of news and political communication, but their role in spreading misinformation has raised significant concerns. In response, these platforms have implemented various content moderation strategies. One such method, Community Notes on X, relies on crowdsourced fact-checking and has gained traction, though it faces challenges such as partisan bias and delays in verification. This study explores an AI-assisted hybrid moderation framework in which participants receive AI-generated feedback —supportive, neutral, or argumentative —on their notes and are asked to revise them accordingly. The results show that incorporating feedback improves the quality of notes, with the most substantial gains resulting from argumentative feedback. This underscores the value of diverse perspectives and direct engagement in human-AI collective intelligence.
The research contributes to ongoing discussions about AI’s role in political content moderation, highlighting the potential of generative AI and the importance of informed design.

\end{abstract}
\keywords{Content Moderation; Human-AI Interaction; Large Language Models; Polarisation; Hybrid Intelligence}

\pagebreak

\section*{Introduction}
The emergence of social media platforms has transformed how users consume and share information. Studies show that these platforms have become primary news sources for many individuals \cite{bakshy2015exposure, sunstein2001echo} and are widely used by politicians as campaign tools \cite{king2017news}. This shift presents challenges for platform owners in monitoring and verifying the information being shared. The widespread dissemination of misleading content and misinformation has become a primary concern for social media platforms. This issue is especially pronounced during significant socio-political events, such as the 2016 U.S. presidential election \cite{allcott2017social} and the Brexit referendum \cite{greene2021misremembering}. The spread of false information during times of crisis can seriously mislead the public. For example, the rapid circulation of misinformation about COVID-19 led the WHO's Director-General to describe the situation as an "infodemic" \cite{who_munich_security_conference}. Another striking case is the January 6th, 2021, attack on the U.S. Capitol, where social media posts helped spread the misinformation that incited violence \cite{klein2025mobilising}. In response to these concerns, social media platforms have implemented various content moderation strategies, including both community-based and automated approaches.

On January 7, 2025, Mark Zuckerberg announced the end of Meta Platforms’ third-party fact-checking program, replacing it with Community Notes \cite{meta2025}. Launched initially on X (formerly Twitter) in January 2021, Community Notes represented the first large-scale, community-based initiative to moderate misleading content \cite{communityNotes}. This shift in moderation strategy reflects the limitations of traditional fact-checking, which relies on human experts to review and classify large volumes of content. This approach is both expensive and challenging to scale \cite{hassan2015quest}. Moreover, expert-driven moderation has faced criticism for potential political and cultural bias. A 2020 report found that 90\% of Republicans in the United States believed social media platforms censor content based on political views \cite{americans2020Vogels}.

Community Notes rely on the wisdom of the crowd to identify misleading content and leverage the diversity of users on the platform to produce moderated outcomes. The process involves displaying specific posts to users in the Community Notes program and asking them to write a note that provides context for those posts. Users are also asked to indicate whether they believe the post is misleading and to provide reasoning for their judgment. Subsequently, other users rate the helpfulness of these notes. Initially, notes with high helpfulness ratings appeared beneath the posts in users’ timelines. However, early analyses revealed strong partisan behaviour. Yasseri and Menczer (2023) found that the network connecting note writers and raters mirrors the highly polarised friendship network on X \cite{yasseri2023can}. Similarly, Allen et al. (2022) showed that users are more likely to write notes on posts by counter-partisans, label them as misleading, and rate notes from counter-partisans as unhelpful, while rating notes from co-partisans as helpful \cite{Allen2022BirdsOther}.

To address partisan behaviour among Community Notes users, adjustments were made to the rating algorithm. Notes that receive helpful ratings from users with diverse perspectives, who typically rate content differently, are now prioritised over those endorsed only by ideologically similar users \cite{communityNotes}. Although this change was intended to reduce polarised interactions, it has increased the time required for notes to be selected and attached to posts. A recent report found that 74\% of accurate Community Notes addressing misinformation about the 2024 U.S. presidential election never reached users, allowing misleading content to go unchallenged. As a result, misinformation posts about the election spread 13 times faster than those accompanied by fact-checked notes \cite{CCDH2024}. These delays undermine the moderation process, as research shows that lag in content moderation significantly reduces its effectiveness \cite{truong2025delayed}.


One approach to addressing the challenges of scale and speed in moderation is the use of automated algorithms. Early efforts in this area have focused on applying machine learning to assess the truthfulness of statements \cite{demartini2020human}. These systems typically rely on supervised learning and require pre-labelled datasets, such as LIAR \cite{wang2017liar}, which compiles fact-checked claims from dedicated websites, and FEVER \cite{thorne2018fever}, which includes claims derived from Wikipedia. However, building high-performing models remains difficult due to the need for large, diverse labelled datasets—a process that demands significant labour from experts or crowdworkers \cite{vidgen2020directions}. The effectiveness of these automated systems is also limited by the quality and biases of their training data, which can perpetuate inaccuracies in classification \cite{binns2017like}.

However, recent advances in generative AI offer promising solutions to the limitations of existing automated content moderation strategies \cite{burton2024, cui2024}. Research shows that Large Language Models (LLMs) can effectively persuade individuals on political issues \cite{bai2023artificial} and generate fact-checking messages that are more persuasive than those produced by human experts \cite{hackenburg2024evaluating}. In addition, several studies demonstrate that LLMs can accurately assess the credibility of news sources \cite{yang2023large} and verify factual claims \cite{quelle2024perils, hoes2023leveraging, kuznetsova2025generative}, with their accuracy improving when fine-tuned on augmented data \cite{zhou2024correcting}. In a recent example, De et al. (2025) fine-tuned an LLM on the Community Notes dataset to generate what they call “supernotes,” which were rated significantly more helpful than notes written by Community Notes users for the same posts \cite{de2025supernotes}. These findings suggest that LLMs are not only capable of understanding complex content but may also outperform human-written moderation in certain cases. However, studies have also found that purely automated approaches can be less effective in the presence of social influence \cite{lu2022effects}. Moreover, people tend to place considerable trust in LLMs, even when the models make mistakes, raising concerns about overreliance on AI-generated content \cite{nguyen2018believe}.

An increasingly supported view in the literature is that the most effective content moderation strategies combine human oversight with AI assistance \cite{gillespie2020content}. This hybrid approach leverages the complementary strengths of humans and machines, enabling moderation that is both more nuanced and scalable \cite{tsvetkova2024}. Schmitt et al. (2024) suggest that models integrating human judgment with AI can outperform either component alone \cite{schmitt2024role}. However, even within hybrid systems, users often overestimate the accuracy of LLMs, placing trust in AI-generated assessments even when they are incorrect—a phenomenon known as automation bias or machine heuristics \cite{cummings2017automation, deverna2024fact}. Moreover, some Human-AI frameworks risk reinforcing users’ existing beliefs, potentially deepening echo chambers and contributing to political polarisation \cite{Jahanbakhsh2023ExploringMedia}. To mitigate these risks, such systems must be carefully designed to avoid unintentionally amplifying bias and division on social media platforms.

Despite these risks, political diversity itself can be a valuable asset in improving community-based moderation efforts. One of the key strengths of Community Notes is its ability to harness ideological diversity—even polarisation—to produce more balanced and neutral notes \cite{kim2025}. A similar dynamic has been observed on Wikipedia, where research attributes the platform's success to the variety of viewpoints among editors, along with its structured policies and guidelines \cite{Arazy2011InformationConflict, Yasseri2012DynamicsWikipedia, Shi2019TheCrowds, yasseri2024computational}. Shi et al. (2019), for instance, found that higher levels of polarisation among editors correlate with higher-quality articles, especially on politically sensitive topics \cite{Shi2019TheCrowds}. Moreover, studies suggest that exposure to cross-partisan messages can reduce both affective and issue-based polarisation \cite{Combs2022AnonymousPlatform, argyle2023leveraging}. These findings indicate that, when properly structured, political diversity not only does not hinder collaboration but also can even enhance it. In a recent study, researchers paired two participants to collaboratively write Community Notes. They found that duos produced more helpful notes than individuals, and that politically diverse groups were especially effective when evaluating posts with Republican-leaning content \cite{juncosa_interplay_2024}. These results further support the idea that political diversity can strengthen the note-writing process in Community Notes, provided it is applied in a way that is both effective and efficient.


Building on these insights, this study proposes a human-AI collaboration framework that uses AI-generated feedback to simulate political diversity in content moderation. The aim is to combine the efficiency of AI with the interpretive strengths of human contributors, while ensuring that humans retain final decision-making authority. We investigate whether AI-generated feedback can emulate cross-partisan collaboration and enhance the quality of contributions on the Community Notes platform. Our focus is on how users revise their notes in response to different types of AI-generated feedback. Specifically, this study addresses the following research questions:


\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{RQ1:} How does the perceived quality of notes vary by the type of feedback received (argumentative, supportive, or neutral)?
\item \textbf{RQ2:} How does the impact of feedback on perceived note quality differ when participants evaluate co-partisan versus cross-partisan posts?
\item \textbf{RQ3:} How does the effect of feedback on perceived quality change based on whether participants believe it was generated by a human expert or an AI model?
\end{itemize}

To investigate these research questions, we conducted an experiment modelled after the Community Notes process. Participants ($N=893$) were shown a post and asked to write a note providing additional context. Each note was then submitted to a GPT-4 language model, which was prompted to generate one of three types of feedback: argumentative, supportive, or neutral. 

Argumentative feedback offers counterpoints to the original note, supportive feedback reinforces the note’s arguments, and neutral feedback simply restates its content. While all feedback was generated by the AI model, we randomly told some participants that the feedback was written by a human expert. This manipulation allows us to examine the roles of human favouritism \cite{morewedge2022preference} and automation bias \cite{cummings2017automation} in shaping participants’ responses.

Participants were then asked to revise their notes in light of the feedback. In a second experiment ($N=1354$), we crowdsourced evaluations of note quality before and after revision. Self-identified Republicans and Democrats rated the notes on a 0–10 scale based on their helpfulness. Figure \ref{fig:experiment_diagram} provides a schematic overview of the experimental workflow.


