To better understand the factors that influence this engagement metric, we analyse the relationship between $FA$ and three key variables: the type of post (supporting either Democratic or Republican ideology), the participant’s self-identified partisanship, and the type of feedback received. The results from the Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression are shown in Table~\ref{tab:ols_FA_no_interaction}.

The analysis shows that users who received argumentative feedback were less likely to incorporate it into their final note than those who received supportive feedback. Additionally, Democratic participants were more likely than Republicans to use the feedback in their final notes. However, the type of post did not have a significant effect on this behaviour.

However, analysing the type of post in isolation may not provide the complete picture. We expect individuals who share the political ideology of the post's author to respond differently when moderating content compared to those with opposing views. To investigate this, we introduce a binary alignment variable that captures the relationship between a participant’s self-identified partisanship and the post’s stance: co-partisan if the participant and the post share the same ideology, and cross-partisan if they do not. A post is classified as co-partisan when it supports Democrats (Republicans) and the participant identifies as a Democrat (Republican); otherwise, it is classified as cross-partisan.


