\end{figure}


\subsection*{Protocol for selecting the Social Media Posts}
We selected 40 different posts from \url{X.com} for the note-writing experiment. These posts were drawn from the Community Notes dataset as of the end of August 2024. The selection followed the criteria below:

\begin{itemize}
\item The post contains at least one of the following keywords: Democrat, Republican, Biden, or Trump.
\item It has at least two notes tagged as "misinformed" in the Community Notes dataset.
\item The post is related to the United States.
\item The text is clear and easy to understand.
\item The post does not include a video or a link to an article required to understand its content.
\end{itemize}

Afterwards, the posts were classified as either pro-Democrat or pro-Republican based on their content. In total, we collected 20 pro-Democrat posts and 20 pro-Republican posts.

\subsection*{Feedback Prompts}
We used the OpenAI API (version 1.25.0) to prompt the GPT-4 model for generating feedback in the note-writing experiment. The "chat.completion" function was employed, with the system message set as: "You are a helpful assistant who writes a note based on the instruction. The note should be limited to 280 characters. Do not use any hashtags or links in your response." The temperature parameter was set to 1. The user message varied depending on the type of feedback. The specific prompts used are as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Argue}: Read the following tweet:{tweet}. Read the comment made in response to the tweet:{comment}. Argue with the comment while maintaining a consistent opposing stance.
    \item \textbf{Support}: Read the following tweet:{tweet}. Read the comment made in response to the tweet: {comment}. Support the comment's point. Help justify this comment, add more arguments if you can.
    \item \textbf{Neutral}: Read the following tweet:{tweet}. Read the comment made in response to the tweet: {comment}. Rephrase the comment. Do not add any evidence or contradict the comment in any way.
\end{itemize}

The "tweet" refers to the content of the post, while the "comment" is created by appending the sentence “The tweet is misleading” or “The tweet is not misleading” to the note written by the participant in the first task. This sentence is selected based on the participant’s classification of the post. The model is explicitly prompted to avoid including links, reducing the risk of hallucinated content generated by the AI.


