\title{\Large\bf  \vspace{-30pt} Simple parallel estimation of the partition ratio \\ for Gibbs distributions}

\author{David G. Harris \\ \normalsize University of Maryland, Department of Computer Science\\ {\normalsize\tt davidgharris29@gmail.com}
 \and Vladimir Kolmogorov \\ \normalsize Institute of Science and Technology Austria \\ {\normalsize\tt vnk@ist.ac.at}}
\date{}
\maketitle

%\vspace{-6pt}
\begin{abstract}
We consider the problem of estimating the partition function $Z(\beta)=\sum_x \exp(\beta(H(x))$ 
of a Gibbs distribution with the Hamiltonian $H:\Omega\rightarrow\{0\}\cup[1,n]$.
As  shown in [Harris \& Kolmogorov 2024], the log-ratio $q=\ln (Z(\betamax)/Z(\betamin))$
can be estimated with accuracy $\eps$ using $O(\frac{q \log n}{\eps^2})$ calls
to an oracle that produces a sample from the Gibbs distribution for parameter $\beta\in[\betamin,\betamax]$.
That algorithm is inherently sequential, or {\em adaptive}: the queried values of $\beta$
depend on previous samples.  Recently, [Liu, Yin \& Zhang 2024] developed a non-adaptive version 
that needs $O( q (\log^2 n) (\log q + \log \log n + \eps^{-2}) )$ samples.

We improve the number of samples to $O(\frac{q \log^2 n}{\eps^2})$ for a non-adaptive algorithm,
and to $O(\frac{q \log n}{\eps^2})$ for an algorithm that uses just two rounds of adaptivity
(matching the complexity of the sequential version).
Furthermore, our algorithm simplifies previous techniques.
In particular, we use just a single estimator, whereas methods in [Harris \& Kolmogorov 2024, Liu, Yin \& Zhang 2024] employ two different estimators for different regimes.

\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
A central problem in computational statistics is to understand the connection between  \emph{sampling} combinatorial objects and  \emph{counting} those objects. We consider sampling/counting algorithms  for Gibbs distributions.  Formally, given a real-valued function $H(\cdot)$ over a finite set $\Omega$,
the {\em Gibbs distribution} is the family of distributions $\mu^{\Omega}_{\beta}$ over $\Omega$, 
parameterized by $\beta$ over an interval $[\betamin, \betamax]$, of the form 
$$
\mu^{\Omega}_\beta(\omega)=\frac{e^{\beta H(\omega)}}{Z(\beta)} 
$$


The normalization factor $Z(\beta)=\sum_{\omega \in\Omega}e^{\beta H(\omega)}$ is called the {\em partition function}. These distributions appear in a number of sampling algorithms and are also common in physics, where the parameter $-\beta$ corresponds to the inverse temperature, and $H(\omega)$ is called the {\em Hamiltonian} of the system. 

Given boundary values $[\betamin, \betamax]$, a key parameter is the partition ratio
$$
Q = \frac{Z(\betamax)}{Z(\beta_{\min})} 
$$

We assume  (after rescaling if necessary)  that we are given known parameters $n, q \geq 2$ with
$$
\log Q \leq q, \qquad  \qquad H(\Omega) \subseteq \{0 \} \cup [1,n]
$$

For example, when $\betamax \leq 0$, we can take $q = \log |\Omega|$ where $|\Omega|$ is the size of the support of the underlying probability space.   

There is an associated distribution which we call the \emph{gross Gibbs distribution} $\mu_{\beta}(x)$ over $\mathbb R$ given by
$$
\mu_{\beta}(x) = \frac{c_x e^{\beta x}}{Z(\beta)}, \qquad \qquad \text{where $c_x = |H^{-1}(x)|$}
$$

For $\beta = -\infty$ we define $\mu_{-\infty}$ to be the distribution which is always zero and we set $Z(-\infty) = c_0$.


\subsection{Estimating the Partition Ratio}
Many counting problems can be recast in terms of the partition ratio $Q$ of a carefully-crafted Gibbs distribution for chosen values $\betamin, \betamax$ (often $\betamin = -\infty$). Our main aim is to develop \emph{black-box} algorithms to estimate $Q$  using oracle access to the gross distribution $\mu_{\beta}$ for chosen query values $\beta \in [\betamin, \betamax]$. No information about the distribution $\mu_{\beta}^{\Omega}$ is used. This allows us obtain counting algorithms from sampling algorithms, in an efficient and unified way. 

The problem of estimation of $Q$ in the oracle setting was first considered in \cite{TPA:journal}, along with an algorithm of sample complexity $O( \frac{q^2}{\eps^2})$. A number of algorithms have been developed since then with steadily improving sample complexities \cite{Bezakova08, Stefankovic:JACM09,Huber:Gibbs,ourpaper}; the best prior algorithm \cite{ourpaper} had cost $O(\frac{q \log n}{\eps^2})$. Also, \cite{ourpaper} showed a near-matching lower bound of $\Omega( \frac{q}{\eps^2} )$.\footnote{If the domain is further assumed to be integer-valued, i.e. $H(\Omega)$ is supported on $\{0, \dots, n \}$, then better upper and lower bounds can be shown in some cases; see \cite{ourpaper} for further details.}

The most efficient estimation algorithms are based on two powerful statistical techniques called the \emph{TPA algorithm} and \emph{Paired Product Estimator (PPE)} developed in \cite{Huber:Gibbs}. One limitation is that the TPA algorithm is inherently sequential, or to put it another way, it is \emph{adaptive}: each query to the sampling oracle depends on the result of previous queries. Thus, these algorithms do not easily lend themselves to parallel algorithms. 

Recently, \cite{liu2024} examined the problem of parallel estimation of $Q$. They developed an algorithm which is completely \emph{non-adaptive} and which uses just $O( q (\log^2 n) (\log q + \log \log n + \eps^{-2}) )$ samples, slightly more than the non-adaptive algorithm.\footnote{We also mention that the algorithm of \cite{liu2024} uses a more restrictive setting than the one we consider: namely, it requires that $\betamax \leq 0$, and sets $q = \log |\Omega|$ throughout. As a point of notation, they use $-\beta$ where we use $\beta$.}  This algorithm, in turn, leads to a number efficient \emph{parallel sampling-to-counting algorithms} (as we discuss shortly). From a broader perspective, it fits into the large-scale question of understanding the role of adaptivity in query tasks. 


 \bigskip

 Our first contribution is to show a much simpler, and slightly more efficient, non-adaptive algorithm for estimating the partition ratio:
\begin{theorem}
\label{mainth1}
There is a non-adaptive sampling algorithm to estimate $Q$ to relative error $\eps$ with probability at least $0.7$ in $O( \frac{q \log^2 n}{\eps^2})$ sample complexity.
\end{theorem}
Instead of multiple algorithms for different parameter ranges, and a complicated non-adaptive schedule, this uses a straightforward schedule and a direct application of the PPE algorithm.

We further show that, with just  \emph{two} rounds of adaptivity, we can match the best serial algorithms.
\begin{theorem}
\label{mainth2}
There is an algorithm using three rounds of sampling to estimate $Q$ to relative error $\eps$ with probability at least $0.7$  in $O(\frac{q \log n}{\eps^2})$ sample complexity.
\end{theorem}

As is usual in estimation problems, the success probability can be augmented to any desired value $1-\delta$ by repeating for $O(\log(1/\delta))$ times and taking the median.


 We leave as an open problem whether a fully-efficient \emph{two-round} sampling algorithm is possible.


\subsection{Computational considerations}
All of our algorithms can easily be implemented in the PRAM model using standard techniques, with polylogarithmic depth and near-linear work factor.  As usual, our algorithms will also work with oracles which can sample \emph{approximately} from the Gibbs distribution, up to some bounded total-variation distance. Such oracles can be achieved from MCMC samplers with polynomial mixing time.

 To simplify and focus the exposition, we will avoid any further discussion of computational issues.

As noted in \cite{liu2024}, there are a number of Gibbs models with parallel sampling algorithms. Combined with \Cref{mainth2}, these immediately turn into efficient counting problems as well. For example, \cite{liu2022simple,chen2025efficient} provides various parallel algorithms for the Ising model and for independent sets in bounded-degree graphs. Our algorithm gives the following computational results, which we quote directly from \cite{liu2024}:
\begin{corollary}
Let $G = (V,E)$ be a graph with maximum degree $\Delta = O(1)$.  There is an algorithm to estimate the partition function of the Ising model or hardcore model in the respective uniqueness regimes with polylogarithmic time complexity and $\tilde O( |E| |V| / \eps^2)$ processor complexity.
\end{corollary}

Note that the small improvement in our query complexity is completely hidden in the $\tilde O$ notation. 


\section{Facts about the Partition Ratio function}
We collect some observations about the behavior of the partition ratio function $Z(\beta)$. This analysis is standard and has appeared in previous works, e.g. \cite{Huber:Gibbs}; however, since these use different notations and may have slightly different parameters, we we include proofs for completeness.

We define $z(\beta) = \log Z(\beta)$ and $z(\beta_1, \beta_2) = z(\beta_2) - z(\beta_1)$.

\begin{proposition}
\label{zprimeprop}
The derivative $z'$ of the function $z$ is given by
$$
z'(\beta) = \frac{ \sum_{x \in \mathbb R} c_x \cdot x e^{\beta x}}{Z(\beta)} = \mathbb E_{X \sim \mu_{\beta}}[X]
$$

It is an increasing function in $\beta$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The formula for $z'(\beta)$ can be verified by direct computation. Also, note that $z''(\beta) = {\mathbb V}\text{ar}_{X \sim \beta}[X]$, which is positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
\label{expprop}
For any values $\alpha, \beta$,  we have $\E_{X \sim \mu_{\beta}}[ e^{\alpha X} ] = \frac{Z(\alpha + \beta)}{Z(\beta)}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Calculate $
\E[ e^{\alpha X}] = \sum e^{\alpha x} \cdot \frac{c_x e^{\beta x}}{Z(\beta)} = \frac{ \sum_x c_x e^{(\alpha + \beta) x} }{Z(\beta)} = \frac{Z(\alpha + \beta)}{Z(\beta)}. $
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
\label{zprimprop1}
If $z'(\beta) \leq 1/2$, then $z(-\infty,\beta) \leq 2 z'(\beta)$
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Here $Z(-\infty) = c_0$, and $Z(\beta) = c_0 + \sum_{x \neq 0} c_x e^{\beta x}$. We have assumed that $\mu_{\beta}$ is supported on $\{0 \} \cup [1,n]$. From \Cref{zprimeprop}, this implies that
$$
Z(\beta) \leq c_0 + \sum_{x} x c_x e^{\beta x}
= c_0 + z'(\beta) Z(\beta) 
$$

So $Z(\beta) \leq \frac{c_0}{1 - z'(\beta)}$ and $z(-\infty, \beta) \leq -\log(1-z'(\beta))$; since $z'(\beta) < 1/2$, this is at most $2 z'(\beta)$.
\end{proof}

One other formula which comes up is the \emph{curvature} defined as:
$$
\kappa(\beta_1, \beta_2) = z(\beta_1) - 2 z( \tfrac{\beta_1 + \beta_2}{2} ) + z(\beta_2)
$$

\begin{proposition}[\cite{Huber:Gibbs}]
\label{kappaa1}
For $\beta_1, \beta_2$, there holds $\kappa(\beta_1, \beta_2) \leq z(\beta_1, \beta_2) \cdot \min \{1, \log( z'(\beta_2) / z'(\beta_1) ) \}.$
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Let $\alpha = \frac{\beta_1 + \beta_2}{2}$. So $\kappa(\beta_1, \beta_2) = z(\alpha, \beta_2) - z(\beta_1, \alpha)$. From monotonicity of $z$, this immediately implies that $\kappa(\beta_1, \beta_2) \leq z(\alpha, \beta_2) \leq z(\beta_1, \beta_2)$. For the second bound, let $v = z'(\beta_2)/z'(\beta_1) \geq 1$. It suffices to show it for $v \leq e$. We use the fact that $z'$ is increasing to estimate:
$$
z(\alpha, \beta_2) \leq z'(\beta_2) (\beta_2 - \alpha), \qquad z(\beta_1, \alpha) \geq z'(\beta_1) (\alpha - \beta_1), \qquad z(\beta_1, \beta_2) \geq z'(\beta_1) (\beta_2 - \beta_1)
$$

Thus,  \begin{align*}
\frac{ \kappa(\beta_1, \beta_2) }{z(\beta_1, \beta_2)} &\leq \frac{z'(\beta_2) (\beta_2 - \alpha) - z'(\beta_1) (\alpha - \beta_1)}{z'(\beta_1)(\beta_2 - \beta_1)} = \frac{z'(\beta_2) - z'(\beta_1)}{2 z'(\beta_1)}  = (v - 1)/2.
\end{align*}

Since $1 \leq v \leq e$, we have $v - 1 \leq 2 \log v$.
\end{proof}




\section{The Paired Product Estimator (PPE)}
The PPE algorithm, introduced in \cite{Huber:Gibbs} is based on a data structure called the \emph{cooling schedule} (we call it just a \emph{schedule} for brevity). Formally, a schedule is a set of values
$$
B = \{ \beta_0 = \betamin, \beta_1, \beta_2, \dots, \beta_t = \betamax \}
$$
with $\beta_0 < \beta_1 < \dots < \beta_t$. The \emph{length} of the schedule is $\text{len}(B) = t+1$,  the \emph{max-width} is defined as
$$
\Delta(B) = \max_{i=0, \dots, t-1} z(\beta_{i},\beta_{i+1})
$$
and the \emph{curvature} is defined as
$$
\kappa(B) = \sum_{i=0}^{t-1} \kappa(\beta_i, \beta_{i+1})
$$

Given a schedule $B$,  the PPE algorithm takes as input an integer parameter $k$, which dictates the number of samples to use. The algorithm will draw $2 k$ non-adaptive samples from each value $\beta_0, \dots, \beta_t$ (slightly fewer for the endpoints).

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\For{$i=0,\ldots,t-1$} {
Draw $k$ samples $X_{i,1}, \dots, X_{i,k}$ from $\mu_{\beta_{i}}$ \\
Draw $k$ samples $Y_{i,1}, \dots, Y_{i,k}$ from $\mu_{\beta_{i+1}}$ \\
Set
$$
U_i = \frac{1}{k} \sum_{j=1}^{k} \exp \bigl( \frac{\beta_{i+1}-\beta_{i}}{2} \cdot X_{i,j} \bigr), \qquad V_i = \frac{1}{k}\sum_{j=1}^{k}\exp \bigl( -\frac{\beta_{i+1}-\beta_{i}}{2} \cdot Y_{i,j} \bigr)
$$
}
\textbf{return} estimate $\hat Q$ given by:
$$
\hat Q = \frac{U_1 U_2 \dots U_t  }{V_1 V_2 \dots V_t}
$$
\caption{\label{alg:PPE-wrapper} Algorithm ${\tt PPE}(B,k)$ for schedule $B$}
\end{algorithm}


\begin{theorem}[\cite{Huber:Gibbs}]
If $\eps \in (0,\tfrac{1}{2})$ and $k \geq \frac{100 (e^{\kappa(B)} - 1)}{\eps^2}$, then the estimate $\hat Q$ produced by ${\tt PPE}(B, k)$ is within relative error $\eps$ of $Q$  with probability at least $0.8$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $i = 0, \dots, t-1$, define $m_i = \frac{\beta_i + \beta_{i+1}}{2}, \kappa_i = \kappa(\beta_i, \beta_{i+1}), U_{i,j} = \exp( \frac{\beta_{i+1} - \beta_i}{2} X_{i,j}), V_{i,j} = \exp( -\frac{\beta_{i+1} - \beta_i}{2} Y_{i,j})$. From \Cref{expprop} and some calculations (see \cite{Huber:Gibbs}), we have:
$$
\E[ U_i ] = \E[ U_{i,j} ] = \frac{Z(m_i)}{Z(\beta_{i})}, \qquad \E[ V_i ] = \E[ V_{i,j}  ] = \frac{Z(m_i)}{Z(\beta_{i+1})},
$$
$$
\frac{\Var[ U_{i,j} ]}{\E[ U_{i,j} ]^2} = \frac{ \Var[V_{i,j}]}{\E[V_{i,j}]^2} =  e^{\kappa_i} - 1,
\qquad
\frac{\Var[ U_i ]}{\E[ U_i ]^2} = \frac{ \Var[V_i]}{\E[V_i]^2} =  (e^{\kappa_i} - 1) / k
$$

Let $U = U_1 \cdots U_t$ and $V = V_1 \cdots V_t$. Since the values $U_i, V_i$ are all independent, we have 
$$
\E[ U ] = \prod_{i=1}^t \frac{Z(m_i)}{Z(\beta_{i})}, \qquad \E[ V  ] = \prod_{i=1}^t \frac{Z(m_i)}{Z(\beta_{i+1})}$$

In particular, by telescoping products, we have:
$$
\frac{\E[U]}{\E[V]} = \prod_{i=1}^t \Bigl(  \frac{Z(m_i)}{Z(\beta_{i})} \cdot \frac{Z(\beta_{i+1})}{Z(m_i)} \Bigr) = \prod_{i=1}^t \frac{Z(\beta_{i+1})}{Z(\beta_{i})} = \frac{Z(\beta_t)}{Z(\beta_0)} = \frac{Z(\betamax)}{Z(\betamin)} = Q.
$$

Also, by the formula for products of relative variances, we calculate:
\begin{equation}
\label{bvv1}
\frac{\Var[ U ]}{\E[U]^2} = \frac{ \Var[V] }{\E[V]^2} =  -1 + \prod_{i=1}^t (1 + (e^{\kappa_i} -1)/k ) 
\end{equation}

Since the function $x \mapsto \log (1 + (e^{x} -1)/k )$ is concave-up, and since $\sum_{i=0}^{t-1} \kappa_i = \kappa(B)$, the RHS of (\ref{bvv1})  is at most $(e^{\kappa(B)} -1)/k$. By specification of $k$, this is at most $\frac{\eps^2}{100}$. So by Chebyshev's inequality, the bounds $|U / \E[U] - 1| \leq \eps / 3$ and $|V / \E[V] - 1| \leq \eps / 3$ each hold with probability at least $0.9$. Suppose they both hold. Then $|\log (U / \E[U])|\le \eps / 2$ and $|\log (V / \E[V])|\le \eps / 2$,
and hence the estimate $\hat Q$ satisfies $|\log \hat Q - \log Q |\le\eps$ as desired.
 \end{proof}

\subsection{Finding a schedule with small curvature}
To use the PPE algorithm, we need an appropriate schedule.  For this, we introduce some definitions.

For $x \in (\betamin, \betamax)$ and a schedule $B = (\beta_0, \dots, \beta_t)$, there is a unique index $v \in \{1, \dots, t-1 \}$ with $x \in [\beta_v, \beta_{v+1})$. We define $B^-(x) = \beta_v$ and $B^+(x) = \beta_{v+1}$. We further define $$
w^+(B,x) = z(x, B^+(x)), \qquad w^-(B,x) = z(B^-(x), x), \qquad w(B,x) =  w^-(B,x) + w^+(B,x)
$$
(the ``width'' of the interval containing $x$). 


The following result is implicit in \cite{ourpaper,Kolmogorov:COLT18} to bound $\kappa$ for a randomly-generated schedule.

\begin{theorem}
\label{kappa-thm}
Let $\theta \in (0,1]$. Consider a random procedure to generate a schedule $B$ such that
$$
\E[w(B, x)] \leq \theta \qquad \qquad \text{for all $x \in (\betamin, \betamax)$}
$$

Then the resulting schedule $B$ satisfies $\E[ \kappa(B) ] \leq 4 \theta \log( n/\theta)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $\alpha$ be such that $z'(\alpha) = \theta/2$. We will show the bound assuming that $\alpha \in (\betamin, \betamax)$; the proof is similar, but simpler, if it is outside this range.

Let $\beta_v = B^-(\alpha)$. By \Cref{kappaa1}, we calculate
\begin{align*}
\kappa(B) &= \sum_{i=0}^v \kappa(\beta_i, \beta_{i+1})  + \sum_{i=v+1}^{t-1} \kappa(\beta_{i}, \beta_{i+1} ) \leq \sum_{i=0}^v z(\beta_i, \beta_{i+1})  + \sum_{i=v+1}^{t-1} z(\beta_{i}, \beta_{i+1} ) \log  \frac{z'(\beta_{i+1})}{z'(\beta_i)}
\end{align*}

Let us consider these two sums in turn. First, by telescoping sums, we have
$$
\sum_{i=0}^v z(\beta_i, \beta_{i+1}) = z(\betamin, \beta_{v+1}) \leq z(\betamin, \beta_v) + w( B, \alpha) \leq z(\betamin, \alpha) + w (B,\alpha)
$$

Next, we expand the second sum as:
\begin{align*}
&\sum_{i=v+1}^{t-1} z(\beta_{i}, \beta_{i+1} ) \log \frac{z'(\beta_{i+1})}{z'(\beta_i)} = \sum_{i=v+1}^{t-1} z(\beta_{i}, \beta_{i+1} ) \int_{y=\beta_i}^{\beta_{i+1}} \frac{z''(y)  \ \mathrm{d}y}{z'(y)}  \\
&\qquad = \sum_{i=v+1}^{t-1}  \int_{y=\beta_i}^{\beta_{i+1}} w(B, y) \frac{z''(y)  \ \mathrm{d}y}{z'(y)} \leq \int_{y=\alpha}^{\betamax} w(B, y) \frac{z''(y)  \ \mathrm{d}y}{z'(y)}
\end{align*}

Using linearity of expectations along with our hypothesis on $\E[w(B,x)]$ for each $x$, this implies:
\begin{align*}
\E[ \kappa(B) ] &\leq z(\betamin, \alpha) + E[ w(B, \alpha) ] + \int_{y=\alpha}^{\betamax} \E[w(B, y)] \frac{z''(y)  \ \mathrm{d}y}{z'(y)} \\
&\leq z(\betamin, \alpha) + \theta + \theta \int_{y=\alpha}^{\betamax} \frac{z''(y) \ \mathrm{d}y}{z'(y)} = z(\betamin, \alpha) + \theta \bigl( 1+  \log \frac{z'(\betamax)}{z'(\alpha)} \bigr)
&\end{align*}

By \Cref{zprimprop1}, and from our choice of $z'(\alpha) = \theta/2$, we have $z(\betamin, \alpha) \leq 2 z'(\alpha) = \theta$. So overall,
$$
\E[ \kappa(B) ] \leq \theta \bigl( 2 + \log(z'(\betamax)/z'(\alpha)) \bigr)
$$

 Finally, observe that $z'(\betamax) \leq n$ and $2 + \log(n/(\theta/2)) \leq  4 \log (n/\theta)$.
\end{proof}
\begin{corollary} 
\label{kappa-cor}
For any schedule $B$ with $\Delta(B) \leq 1$, there holds $\kappa(B) \leq 4 \Delta(B) \log( n/\Delta(B))$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Clearly $w(B,x) \leq \Delta(B)$ for all $x$. So, the schedule $B$ satisfies \Cref{kappa-thm} with $\theta = \Delta(B)$. \end{proof}

\subsection{Putting it together}
We now describe the overall algorithm flow, both generating the schedule and using it for PPE. 

As a starting point, we can use a non-adaptive schedule adapted from \cite{liu2024}; since our parameters are slightly different, we provide a proof as well as a simpler construction later in \Cref{sec:nonadapt}.
\begin{theorem}[\cite{Bezakova08,liu2024}]
\label{non-adapt}
For $\theta \in (0,1]$, the algorithm $ {\tt NonAdapativeSchedule}(\theta)$ generates a schedule of length $O( \frac{q \log(n/\theta)}{\theta} )$ and max-width at most $\theta$. The algorithm does not sample from the oracle.
\end{theorem}  

\Cref{kappa-cor} and \Cref{non-adapt} together immediately gives us \Cref{mainth1}.
\begin{proof}[Proof of \Cref{mainth1}, given \Cref{non-adapt}]
Set $B = {\tt StaticSchedule}( \frac{1}{4 \log n})$, with $\len(B) \leq O( q \log^2 n)$ and $\kappa(B) \leq \frac{4}{ 4 \log n} \log(4 n \log n) \leq  3$. Then run ${\tt PPE}(B,k)$ with $k = \frac{100 (e^{3}-1)}{\eps^2}$. 
\end{proof}

To get the three-round algorithm, we develop a parallel sampling procedure which is inspired by the TPA process.  The full details are provided later in \Cref{sec:tpa}; we summarize it as follows:
\begin{theorem}
\label{prune-alg}
The algorithm ${\tt PseudoTPA}(\theta)$ takes as input a parameter $\theta \in (0,1]$, and produces a random schedule $B$, with the following properties:
\begin{itemize}
\item The expected number of samples used by the algorithm is $O(q/\theta + q \log n)$, over two rounds of adaptive sampling.
\item The expected length of the schedule $B$ is $O(q/\theta)$.
\item For any $x \in (\betamin, \betamax)$, there holds $\E[ w(B, x) ] \leq \theta$.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof of \Cref{mainth2}, given \Cref{prune-alg}]
Generate the schedule $B = {\tt PseudoTPA}(\frac{1}{4 \log n})$. Then run ${\tt PPE}(B,k)$ with $k = \frac{100 (e^{30}-1)}{\eps^2}$. 

By \Cref{kappa-thm}, we have $\E[ \kappa(B) ] \leq \frac{4}{4 \log n} \cdot \log (4 n \log n) \leq 3$ and $\E[ \len(B) ] \leq O(q \log n)$. The expected sample complexity of {\tt PseudoTPA} is $O(q \log n)$ and the expected sample complexity of {\tt PPE} is $\E[ \len(B) \cdot k]= O(\frac{ q \log n}{\eps^2})$.

By Markov's inequality, $\Pr{ \kappa(B) \leq 30} \geq 0.9$. When this event holds,  the estimate produced by {\tt PPE} is good with probability at least $0.8$. Overall, the estimate is good with probability at least $0.7$.  
\end{proof}

\section{The non-adaptive schedule}
\label{sec:nonadapt}
The original algorithm for generating a non-adaptive schedule appeared in \cite{Bezakova08}. Here, we present a simplified and generalized version of it. It will also be used later in our adaptive algorithm.


\begin{algorithm}[H]
\DontPrintSemicolon
set $\beta_0 = \betamax$ \\
\For {$i=0$ {\bf to} $+\infty$}
{
   set $s_i = \min \{n, \frac{q}{\betamax - \beta_i} \}$      \\
   set $\beta_{i+1} = \beta_i - \theta/s_i$ \\
   \If{$s_i < \theta/2$ or $\beta_{i+1} < \betamin$} {
      \textbf{return} schedule $B = \{ \betamin, \beta_i, \beta_{i-1}, \dots, \beta_{1}, \beta_0 = \betamax \}$ 
      }
}  
\caption{${\tt StaticSchedule}(\theta)$.\label{alg:non1}}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{proposition}
\label{derivprop}
In each iteration $i$ of Algorithm~\ref{alg:non1}, there holds $z'(\beta_i) \leq s_i$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
First, since $z'(\beta)$ is the expectation of $X \sim \mu_{\beta}$, and $\mu_{\beta}$ is supported on $\{ 0 \} \cup [1, n]$, we clearly have $z'(\beta) \leq n$. Also, since $z'(\beta)$ is increasing, we have $z(\betamax) \geq z(\beta_i) + z'(\beta_i) (\betamax - \beta_i)$.

By hypothesis, $q$ is an upper bound on $z(\betamax) - z(\betamin)$. Since $\beta_i > \betamin$ (else the algorithm would have terminated at an earlier iteration), this implies that $z(\beta_i, \betamax) \leq q$. Hence $z'(\beta_i) \leq \frac{q}{\betamax - \beta_i}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
The schedule $B = {\tt StaticSchedule}(\theta)$ has $\Delta(B) \leq \theta$. 
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
First, observe that since $z'$ is an increasing function,  we have $z(\beta_{i+1}, \beta_{i}) \leq (\beta_{i} - \beta_{i+1}) z'(\beta_i) = (\theta/s_i) \cdot z'(\beta_i)$. This is at most $\theta$ by \Cref{derivprop}.

If the algorithm terminates due to the condition $\beta_{i+1} < \betamin$, this implies also that $z(\betamin, \beta_i) \leq \theta$. Otherwise, if the algorithm terminates due to the condition, then by \Cref{zprimprop1}, this implies that $z(-\infty, \beta_{i}) \leq 2 z'(\beta_i) \leq 2 s_i \leq \theta$ as desired.
\end{proof}


\begin{proposition}
The schedule $B = {\tt StaticSchedule}(\theta)$ has length $O( \frac{q \log(n/\theta)}{\theta} )$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
For the initial iterations of the algorithm, where $\beta_i \geq \betamax - q/n$, we have $s_i = n$ and $\beta_{i+1} = \beta_i - \theta/n$. There are at most $\frac{q/n}{\theta/n} = q/\theta$ iteration with this property. 

Next, let $j$ denote the first iteration where $\beta_i < \betamax - q/n$. For the remaining iterations $i > j$ of this algorithm, we have $s_i = \frac{q}{\betamax - \beta_i}$. Thus, after this point, observe that
$$
s_{i+1} = \frac{q}{\betamax - \beta_{i+1}} = \frac{q}{\betamax - \beta_i + \theta/s_i} = \frac{q}{\betamax - \beta_i + \theta/(q/(\betamax - \beta_i))} = s_i \cdot \frac{q}{q + \theta}
$$


Thus, for $i > j$, we have $s_{i} \leq n \bigl( \frac{q}{q + \theta} \bigr)^{i-j}$. So iteration $i = j + \frac{\log n - \log(\theta/2)}{\log (q+\theta) - \log q}$ has $s_i < \theta/2$ and the algorithm terminates. As $q>2$ and $\theta \in (0,1]$, this is $O( \frac{q \log(n/\theta)}{\theta} )$.
\end{proof}


\section{The TPA algorithm and its parallel counterpart}
\label{sec:tpa}

In this section, we write $\mathcal E$ for the unit-rate Exponential probability distribution.

To begin, we recall the \emph{TPA algorithm} of \cite{Huber:Gibbs}. It is based on the following procedure, developed in \cite{tpa} (which is designed for even more general statistical estimation tasks, beyond just Gibbs distributions):


\begin{proposition}[\cite{tpa}]
\label{exponentialprop}
Let $\beta \in \mathbb R$. Suppose $X \sim \mu_{\beta}$ and $\eta \sim \mathcal E$. Then the random variable $z( \beta - \eta/X, \beta)$ also has the distribution $\mathcal E$. (For $X = 0$ we define $\beta - \eta/X = -\infty$)
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
 We need to show that $\Pr{ z(\beta - \eta/X, \beta) > t} = e^{-t}$ for any $t$, to match the CDF of $\mathcal E$. Let $\alpha$ be such that $z(\alpha, \beta) = t$. So $\Pr{z(\beta - \eta/X, \beta) > t} = \Pr{ \beta - \eta/X < \alpha } = \Pr{ \eta > (\beta - \alpha) X}$.  By definition of $\eta$, we have $\Pr{ \eta > (\beta-\alpha) X \mid X} = e^{-(\beta - \alpha) X}$. By iterated expectations along with \Cref{expprop}, this implies that $\Pr{ \eta > (\beta-\alpha) X } = \E[ e^{-(\beta-\alpha) X}] =  Z(\alpha)/Z(\beta)$. This is precisely $e^{-z(\alpha, \beta)} = e^{-t}$.
\end{proof}

In light of \Cref{exponentialprop}, \cite{Huber:Gibbs} defined the TPA process to generate a schedule in the Gibbs context:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\DontPrintSemicolon
set $\beta_0 = \betamax$ \\
\For {$i=0$ {\bf to} $+\infty$}
{
   draw $X$ from $\mu_{\beta_{i}}$  and draw $\eta$ from $\mathcal E$. \\
   set $\beta_{i+1} = \beta_{i} - \eta / X$. \\
  \textbf{if  $\beta_{i+1} \leq \betamin$ then return} schedule $
  B= \{ \betamin, \beta_{i}, \beta_{i-1}, \dots, \beta_0 = \betamax \}.
  $
}  
\caption{ {The TPA process. {\tt Output:} a schedule over interval $[\betamin,\betamax]$}}
\label{alg:TPAone0}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{lemma}[\cite{Huber:Gibbs}]
Suppose we run $k$ independent executions of the TPA Process, resulting in schedules $B_1, \dots, B_k$. Then the combined schedule $B = B_1 \cup \dots \cup B_k$  has expected length $k q + 2$, and satisfies \Cref{kappa-thm} with parameter $\theta = 2/k$. 
(This schedule is called the ${\tt TPA}(k)$ process.)
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}

By \Cref{exponentialprop}, the distances between values $z(\beta_i)$ in each schedule $B_j$ follow an Exponential distribution; this is precisely the distribution of inter-arrival times in a Poisson Point Process (PPP).  Thus, for each $j$, the values $z(B_j) \cap (\betamin, \betamax)$  form a rate-one PPP. By the superposition principle, the union $z(B) \cap (\betamin, \betamax)$ forms a rate-$k$ PPP. The expected number of points is $k q$.

For any given $x \in (\betamin, \betamax)$, the waiting time between $z(x)$ and the next-largest value $z( B^+(x) )$ is an Exponential random variable of rate $k$ (or it is capped at the maximum value $z(x,\betamax)$). In a completely symmetric way, the waiting time to the next-smallest value $z(B^-(x))$ is an Exponential random variable of rate $k$. So both $W^+(B,x) = z(x, B^+(x))$ and $W^-(B,x) = z(B^-(x), x)$ are rate-$k$ Exponential random variables, both of mean $1/k$. 
\end{proof}

Unfortunately, the TPA process is inherently sequential: each computed value $\beta_{i+1}$ depends on a draw from the previous distribution $\mu_{\beta_{i}}$. This leads us to introduce Algorithm~\ref{alg2}, which can be seen as a ``local approximate'' version of ${\tt TPA}(k)$.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
Set parameters $d = 2, \theta' = 1/4, k = \lceil 8/\theta \rceil$. \\
Set $B = \{\beta_0 = \betamin, \beta_1, \beta_2, \dots, \beta_{t-1}, \beta_t = \betamax \} = {\tt StaticSchedule}(\theta')$  \\
Initialize $B' = B'' = \{ \betamin, \betamax \}$ \\
\For{each $i = 1, \dots, t-1$} {
Draw $X_{i,1}, \dots, X_{i,d} \sim \mu_{\beta_{i+1}}$ \\
Place $\beta_{i}$ into schedule $B'$ with probability $1 -  e^{-(X_{i,1} + \dots + X_{i,d}) (\beta_{i+1} - \beta_{i})}$.
}
\For{each $\beta_i \in B'$}{
Draw $\eta_{i,1}, \dots, \eta_{i,k} \sim \mathcal E$ and draw $Y_{i,1}, \dots, Y_{i,k} \sim \mu_{\beta_i}$ \\
Add $\beta_i$ and $\beta_i - \eta_{i,j}/Y_{i,j}: j = 1, \dots, k$ into final schedule $B''$ \\
}
\textbf{return} schedule $B'' \cap [\betamin, \betamax]$
\caption{\label{alg2} Algorithm ${\tt PseudoTPA}(\theta)$}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{proposition}
\label{fr1prop}
For any $i \in \{1, \dots, t-1 \}$, we have $\beta_i \in B'$ with probability $1 - (Z(\beta_i)/Z(\beta_{i+1}))^d$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Taking expectation over the random variables $X_{i+1,j}$ we have $$
\Pr{\beta_i  \in B' } = 1 - \E[ e^{-(X_{i,1} + \dots + X_{i,d}) (\beta_{i+1} - \beta_i)} ] = 1 -  \prod_{j=1}^d \E[e^{-X_{i,j} (\beta_{i+1} - \beta_{i})}].
$$
Now apply \Cref{expprop}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
\label{lenprop1}
$\E[ \len(B')] \leq d q + 2$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Observe that $\sum_{i=1}^{t-1} \Pr{\beta_i \in B'} = \sum_{i=1}^{t-1} \Bigl( 1-\bigl( \frac{ Z(\beta_i)}{Z(\beta_{i+1})} \bigr)^d \Bigr) = \sum_{i=1}^{t-1} (1-e^{-d z(\beta_i, \beta_{i+1})})$.

Using the inequality $1-e^{-a} \leq a$ for $a \geq 0$, we thus have $\E[\len(B')] \leq 2 + \sum_{i=1}^{t-1} d z(\beta_i, \beta_{i+1})$. By telescoping sums, this equals $2 + d ( z(\beta_t) - z(\beta_1)) \leq 2 + d q$.
\end{proof}

In light of \Cref{lenprop1}, the schedule $B''$ has expected length $\E[ \len(B') \cdot (k+1) ]= O( k q )$ (bearing in mind that $d$ is constant). The algorithm uses two rounds of adaptive sampling: Line 5 uses $d \len(B) = O( q \log n)$ samples and Line 8 uses $k \len(B') = O (k q )$ samples in expectation. 

The key task is to bound the curvature of the schedule $B''$. We do this in two stages. First, we show that the schedule $B'$ has interval widths $w(B',x)$ which are tightly concentrated around $O(1)$;  second, we show that $B''$ subdivides each such interval into roughly $k$ equal subintervals.

\begin{proposition}
\label{trab1}
Let $x \in (\betamin, \betamax)$ and $s > 0$. We have
$$
\Pr{w^+(B',x) > w^+(B,x) + s} \leq e^{-d s}, \qquad \Pr{ w^-(B',x) > w^-(B,x) + s} \leq e^{-d s}
$$
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The two bounds are completely symmetric, so we show only the bound on $w^+(B',x)$.

Let $\beta_i = B^+(x)$, and let index $v < t$ be maximal such that $z(\beta_i,\beta_v) \leq s$; if there is no such index, then since $\betamax \in B'$ we have $w^+(B',x) \leq w^+(B,x) + s $ with probability one. 

Now $w^+(B',x) > w^+(B,x) + s$ only if $B' \cap \{ \beta_i, \beta_{i+1}, \dots, \beta_v \} = \emptyset$. By \Cref{lenprop1}, and using independence of each iteration $i$, we calculate
$$
\Pr{ B' \cap \{ \beta_i, \beta_{i+1}, \dots, \beta_v \} = \emptyset } = \prod_{j=i}^{v} \Pr{ \beta_j \notin B'}  = \prod_{j=i}^{v}  (\frac{Z(\beta_j)}{Z(\beta_{j+1})})^d  = (\frac{Z(\beta_i)}{Z(\beta_{v + 1})})^d = e^{-d \cdot z(\beta_i, \beta_{v+1})}
$$

 By definition of $v$, we have $z(\beta_i, \beta_{v+1}) \geq s$.
 \end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
\label{trab2}
Let $x \in (\betamin, \betamax)$ and $s > 0$. If we condition on the schedule $B'$, we have
\begin{align*}
\Pr{w^+(B'', x) > s} &\leq \bigl( 1 - e^{-w^+(B,x)}(e^s - 1) \bigr)^k \\
\Pr{w^-(B'', x) > s} &\leq \bigl( 1 - e^{-w^+(B,x)}(1 - e^{-s}) \bigr)^k
\end{align*}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Define the following parameters:
\begin{align*}
&\beta_{i^+} = (B')^+(x) \\
&r = z(x, \beta_{i^+}) = w^+(B',x)  \\
&\alpha_j = \beta_{i^+} - \eta_{i^+, j}/Y_{i^+,j} \qquad \qquad \text{for $j = 1, \dots, k$}  \\
&V_j = z(\alpha_j, \beta_{i^+}) \qquad  \quad \qquad \qquad \text{for $j = 1, \dots, k$}
\end{align*}

We have $w^+(B'', x) > s$ only if $[z(x),z(x)+s] \cap \{ z(\alpha_1), \dots, z(\alpha_k) \} = \emptyset$, since each point $\alpha_j$ gets placed into $B''$. Subtracting from $z(\beta_{i^+})$, this implies that $[r - s, r] \cap \{ V_1, \dots, V_k \} = \emptyset$.  By \Cref{exponentialprop}, each $V_j$ is a unit-rate Exponential random variable, and they are all independent. So
$$
\Pr{ [r - s, r] \cap \{ V_1, \dots, V_k \} = \emptyset } = (1 + e^{-r} - e^{s-r})^k = (1 - e^{-r}(e^s - 1))^k
$$
which establishes the first claim.

Similarly, $w^-(B'', x) > s$ only if $[z(x)-s,z(x)] \cap \{ z(\alpha_1), \dots, z(\alpha_k) \} = \emptyset$. Subtracting from $z(\beta_{i^+})$, this implies that $[r, r + s] \cap \{ V_1, \dots, V_k \} = \emptyset$. Again, by \Cref{exponentialprop}, we have
\[
\Pr{ [r, r + s] \cap \{ V_1, \dots, V_k \} = \emptyset } = (1 - e^{-r} + e^{-r-s})^k = (1 - e^{-r}(1 - e^{-s}))^k. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
\label{trar2}
Suppose we condition on the schedule $B'$. Then for any $x \in (\betamin, \betamax)$ we have
$$
\E[ w^+(B'', x) ] \leq \frac{e^{w^+(B', x)}}{k}, \qquad \E[ w^-(B'', x) ] \leq \frac{e^{w(B', x)}}{k}
$$
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Let $r =  w^+(B',x), p = w^-(B',x)$. 

Observe that $w^+(B'',x) \leq w(B',x) = r$ with probability one, since $B' \subseteq B''$. Thus, by \Cref{trab2}, can write
$$
\E[ w^+(B'', x) ] = \int_{s=0}^{r} \Pr{ w^+(B'',x) > s } \ \mathrm{d}s \leq \int_{s=0}^{r}  (1 - e^{-r}(e^s - 1))^k \ \mathrm{d}s
$$

Using the bound $1+a \leq e^a$ twice, we observe that $$
1 - e^{-r} (e^s - 1) \leq e^{-s e^{-r}}
$$

Plugging this back into our estimate for $\E[w^+(B'',x)]$ gives
$$
\E[ w^+(B'',x) ] \leq \int_{s=0}^{\infty} ( e^{-s e^{-r}} )^k \ \mathrm{d}s = \frac{e^{r}}{k}
$$
which establishes the first claim.

Similarly, $w^-(B'',x) \leq w^-(B',x) = p$ with probability one, so \Cref{trab2} gives:
$$
\E[ w^-(B'',x) ] = \int_{s=0}^{p} \Pr{ w^-(B'',x) > s} \leq \int_{s=0}^{p}  (1 - e^{-r}(1-e^{-s}))^k \ \mathrm{d}s 
$$

Since $s \in [0,p]$, we have $1 - e^{-s} \geq s e^{-p}$.
This, together with the bound $1+a \leq e^a$ implies that $$
1 - e^{-r} (1 - e^{-s}) \leq e^{-s e^{-r - p}}
$$

Plugging this into to the bound for $w^-(B'',x)$, we conclude that
\[
\E[ w^-(B'',x) ] \leq \int_{s=0}^{\infty} e^{-k s e^{-r-p}} \ \mathrm{d}s = \frac{e^{p+r}}{k}. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

Putting these calculations together gives the following:
\begin{proposition}
For any $x \in (\betamin, \betamax)$, we have $\E[w(B'',x)] \leq 12/k \leq \theta$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
By \Cref{trab1}, we can calculate
\begin{align*}
\E[ e^{w^+(B',x)} ] &= e^{w^+(B,x)}  \Bigl( 1 + \int_{s=0}^{\infty} \Pr{ w^+(B',x) > w^+(B,x) + s} e^s \ \mathrm{d}s \Bigr) \\
&\leq e^{w^+(B,x)} \Bigl( 1 +  \int_{s=0}^{\infty} e^{-2 s} \cdot  e^s \ \mathrm{d}s \Bigr) = 2 e^{w^+(B,x)}. 
\end{align*}

By a symmetric argument, we also have $\E[e^{w^-(B',x)}]  \leq 2 e^{w^-(B,x)}$. Since $w^+(B',x)$ and $w^-(B,x)$ are determined by independent variables, this implies that $$
\E[ e^{w(B', x)} ] \leq \E[ e^{w^+(B', x)}]  \E[ e^{w^-(B', x)}] \leq 4 e^{w(B,x)}.
$$

At this point, observe that $\Delta(B) \leq \theta' = 1/4$ and so $w^+(B,x) \leq w(B,x) \leq 1/4$. So $$
\E[e^{w^+(B',x)}] \leq 2 e^{1/4} \leq 2.6, \qquad \text{ and } \qquad \E[e^{w(B',x)]} \leq 4 e^{1/4} \leq 5.2.
$$

Plugging these estimates into \Cref{trar2} and using iterated expectations, we have
\begin{align*}
\E[w^+(B'', x)] &\leq \E[ e^{w^+(B', x)} ]/k \leq 2.6/k \\
\E[w^-(B'', x)] &\leq \E[ e^{w(B', x)} ]/k \leq 5.2/k
\end{align*}

So overall,  $\E[w(B'', x)] = \E[w^-(B'', x)] + \E[w^+(B'', x)] \leq 7.8/k$. Our choice $k = \lceil 8/\theta \rceil$ ensures that this at most $\theta$.
\end{proof}

This concludes the proof of \Cref{prune-alg}.

