\begin{abstract}
Finding an Approximate Longest Common Substring (ALCS) within a given set $\s{S}=\{s_1,s_2,\ldots,s_m\}$ of $m \ge 2$ strings is a problem of particular importance in computational biology (e.g., identifying related mutations across multiple genetic sequences).
In this paper, we study several ALCS problems that, for given integers $k$ and $t \le m$, require finding a longest string $u$ --- or a longest substring $u$ of any string in $\s{S}$ --- that lies within distance $k$ of at least one substring in $t$ distinct strings of $\s{S}$. Although two of these problems, denoted $k$-LCS and \textit{k-t} LCS, are NP-hard, nevertheless restricted variations of them under Hamming and edit distance can be solved in $\bigo(N^2)$ and $\bigo(k\ell N^2)$ time, respectively, where $\ell$ is the length of each string and $N=m\ell$. Further, we show that using the $k$-errata tree data structure, a restricted variation of the ALCS problem under both Hamming and edit distance can be computed in $\bigo(mN\log^k \ell)$ time.
We also establish an $\bigo(N^2)$ conditional lower bound for a variation of the ALCS problem under the Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis. 
\keywords{Approximate Longest Common Substring \and LCS of Multiple Strings \and Hamming Distance \and Edit Distance \and k-errata Trie \and Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis}
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Finding similar strings occurring within a specified distance from a given string set is a challenging problem in many application areas, notably in the analysis of genomic sequences in computational biology. The Longest Common Substring (LCS) is widely used to quantify similarity among DNA sequences. However, due to the evolutionary processes affecting biological sequences, instead of finding exact common substrings, ``approximate'' occurrences --- such as the Approximate Longest Common Substring (ALCS) --- are of great interest. 

Exact applications of the LCS problem are not always useful for measuring the similarity between two or more sequences. For example, let $ELCS(s_1,s_2)$ denote the Exact LCS between strings $s_1$ and $s_2$. For given strings $s_1=a^mba^m$, $s_2=a^{2m}b$, and $s_3=ba^{2m}$, we find $ELCS(s_1,s_2)=ELCS(s_1,s_3)=a^mb$, while $ELCS(s_2,s_3) = a^{2m}$. But the similarity of $s_1$ with $s_2$ also relates to $a^{2m}$. To more accurately address these similarities, several problems related to the ALCS of two strings have been defined~\cite{Abboud2015MoreDesign,Babenko2011ComputingMismatch,Flouri2015LongestMismatches,Grabowski2015AProblem,Thankachan2016AProblem}. In this paper, we study ALCS problems for more than two strings.

In Section~\ref{sec:prelimin}, we introduce terminology. Then, in Section~\ref{sec:DefforApproxLCS} we define new problems related to the ALCS of multiple strings. In Section~\ref{sec:litReview} we review existing literature on LCS. In Section~\ref{sec:main} we show NP-hardness of the $k$-LCS and $kt$-LCS problems and present polynomial time and polylog time solutions to the \textit{restricted} ALCS problems. Finally, in Section~\ref{sec:seth}, we present SETH-hardness of the $Rk$-LCS problem.


\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:prelimin}

A \bo{string} $s$ is a sequence of $n \geq 0$ \bo{letters} drawn from a finite ordered set $\Sigma$ called an \bo{alphabet}. Alphabet \bo{size} is $\sigma = |\Sigma|$. Thus, we treat a string $s$ as an array $s[1..n]$ of elements drawn from $\Sigma$. We say that $s$ has \bo{length} $n=|s|$ and \bo{positions} $1,2,3,\ldots,n$. An \bo{empty} string $(n=0)$ is denoted by $\s{\varepsilon}$.
For any pair of integers $i$ and $j$ satisfying $1 \leq i \leq j \leq n$, we define a \bo{substring} $s[i..j]$ (sometimes called \bo{factor}) of $s$ as follows: $s[i..j]=s[i]s[i+1]..s[j]$. We say that substring $s_1[i..j]$ of $s_1$ \bo{occurs} in string $s_2$ if a substring 
$s_2[i'..j'] = s_1[i..j]$. A \bo{prefix} (\bo{suffix}) of a nonempty $s$ is a substring $s[i..j]$, where $i=1$ $(j=n)$.

An \bo{edit operation} on a string is the insertion, deletion or substitution of a single letter.
The \bo{cost} of an edit operation is an integer function of the operation type and the letters involved.
Given two strings $s_1$ and $s_2$, both of length $n$, the \bo{Hamming distance} $d_H(s_1,s_2)$, is defined to be the minimum cost of substitutions that transform $s_1$ to $s_2$. For any two strings $s_1$ and $s_2$ of arbitrary lengths, the \bo{edit distance} $d_{E}(s_1, s_2)$ is
the minimum cost over all sequences of edit operations that transform $s_1$ to $s_2$.
In the case that each edit operation has unit cost, the edit distance is called \bo{Levenshtein distance} and denoted $d_{L}(s_1, s_2)$.
A \bo{k-approximate occurrence} of any string $u$ under distance 
$\delta \in \{H,L,E\}$ 
is a string $u'$ such that $d_{\delta}(u,u') \leq k$.

\section{Definitions of ALCS Problems on Multiple Strings}\label{sec:DefforApproxLCS}

In this section, we define the following problems on a given alphabet $\Sigma$ related to the ALCS of multiple strings under distance metric $\delta$,  where $\delta \in \{H, L, E\}$ represents Hamming, Levenshtein, and edit distances, respectively, and a maximum distance $k > 0$ is specified. For given finite $k, t, m \in \mathbb{N}$, $t\leq m$, a set $\s{S}=\{s_1, s_2, \ldots , s_m\}$ of strings, where $|s_1|=|s_2|=\ldots=|s_m|=\ell$, we define the following problems:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{\textit{k} Longest Common Substring (\textit{k}-LCS)}: find a longest string $u$ such that there exist  substrings $u_i$ of $s_i$, $1 \le i \le m$, for which $d_\delta(u,u_i) \le k$.
    \item \textbf{\textit{k-t} Longest Common Substring (\textit{k-t} LCS)}: find a longest string $u$ such that there exists a subset $\s{S}' = \{s'_1,s'_2,\ldots,s'_t\} \subseteq \s{S}$, for which substrings $u'_i$ of $s'_i$, $1 \le i \leq t$, satisfy $d_\delta(u,u'_i) \le k$.
    \item \textbf{Restricted \textit{k} Longest Common Substring (\textit{Rk}-LCS)}: find a longest substring $u$ of any string in \s{S}, such that there exist substrings $u_i$ of $s_i$, $1 \le i \le m$, for which $d_\delta(u,u_i) \le k$.
    \item \textbf{Restricted \textit{k-t} Longest Common Substring (\textit{Rkt}-LCS)}: find a longest substring $u$ of any string in \s{S} such that a subset $\s{S}' = \{s'_1,s'_2,\ldots,s'_t\} \subseteq \s{S}$ exists for which substrings $u'_i$ of $s'_i$, $1 \le i \leq t$, satisfy $d_\delta(u,u'_i) \le k$.
    \item \textbf{Max Coverage \textit{Rkt}-LCS}: find the maximum size set $\s{U}=\{u_1, u_2, \ldots , u_q\}$, where each $u_i$, $1 \leq i \leq q$, is a \textit{Rkt}-LCS of $\s{S}$ under metric $\delta$.
    \item \textbf{Max Set \textit{Rkt}-LCS}: given a string $u$, which is an \textit{Rkt}-LCS of $\s{S}$ under distance metric $\delta$, find the maximum size set $\s{S}' = \{s'_1,s'_2,\ldots,s'_p\} \subseteq \s{S}$, $p \geq t$, such that there exist substrings $u'_i$ of $s'_i$, $1 \le i \leq p$, satisfying $d_\delta(u,u'_i) \le k$.
\end{enumerate}

In the first two problems ($k$-LCS and $k$-$t$ LCS), the output is a longest string drawn from $\Sigma$, while, in the \textit{restricted} variations of these problems ($Rk$-LCS and $Rkt$-LCS), we require the solution to be a longest substring of one of the strings in $\s{S}$. For $\s{S}=\{s_1,s_2,s_3,s_4\} = \{aabcf,fabcd,dgiabc,ahabch\}$, $k=2$, and $t=3$, substring $iabc$ of $s_3$ is a $Rk$-LCS of \s{S}, substring $aabcf$ of $s_1$ is a $Rkt$-LCS of \s{S}, substring set $U=\{aabcf,habch,fabcd\}$ is the Max Coverage \textit{Rkt}-LCS of \s{S}, and the Max Set \textit{Rkt}-LCS of \s{S} for the $Rkt$-LCS $habch$ is  $\s{S}' = \{s_1, s_2, s_4\}$.

\section{Literature Review}\label{sec:litReview}

In this section, we review the three following categories of LCS problems. These problems can be regarded as variations of the problems defined in Section~\ref{sec:DefforApproxLCS}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item ELCS of two strings \textit{(\textit{Rk}-LCS, where \textit{k=0} and \textit{m=2})} 
    \item ELCS of more than two strings 
    \textit{(\textit{Rkt}-LCS, where \textit{k=0} and $t>2$)}
    \item ALCS of two strings \textit{(\textit{Rk}-LCS, where $k> 0$ and \textit{m=2})}
\end{itemize}
\textbf{ELCS of two strings.} Given two strings $s_1$ and $s_2$, each of length $n$, find a longest substring occurring in both $s_1$ and $s_2$.

Weiner showed that this problem can be solved in $\bigo(n)$-time for a constant-size alphabet~\cite{Weiner1973LinearAlgorithms}.
Later, Farach showed that if the alphabet is not constant, a suffix tree can be constructed in linear time in addition to the time required for sorting its letters~\cite{Farach1997OptimalAlphabets}. This yielded an $\bigo(n)$-time algorithm for the ELCS problem in the word RAM model for polynomially-bounded integer alphabets. Then, using Farach's results, Charalampopoulos \textit{et al.} showed that the ELCS problem over an alphabet $[0,\sigma)$ can be solved in $\bigo(n \log \sigma/ \sqrt{\log n})$-time and $\bigo(n/ \log_\sigma n)$-space complexity, where $\log \sigma = o(\sqrt{\log n})$~\cite{Charalampopoulos2021FasterSubstring}.
\newline
\textbf{ELCS of more than two strings.} Given a string set $\s{S}=\{s_1, s_2, \ldots , s_m\}$, $m$ positive integers $x_1,x_2,..,x_m$, and $t$ where $1 \leq t \leq m$, find a longest substring $u$ of any string in \s{S} for which there are at least $t$ strings $s_{i_1},s_{i_2},\ldots,s_{i_t}$ $(1 \leq i_1 < i_2 <\ldots< i_t \leq m)$ such that $u$ occurs at least $x_{i_j}$ times in $s_{i_j}$ for each $j$ with $1 \leq j \leq t$~\cite{Arnold2011LinearProblem}. 

Lee and Pinzon show that this problem can be solved in $\bigo(N)$ time~\cite{Lee2007ARepeats}, where $N=\sum_{i=1}^{m}|s_{i}|$. A simple solution for all $1 \leq t \leq m$, requires $\bigo(mN)$ time using Lee and Pinzon results. However, a much simpler and more efficient solution was proposed by Arnold and Ohlebusch that requires $\bigo(N)$ time for all $1 \leq t \leq m$~\cite{Arnold2011LinearProblem}.\newline
\textbf{ALCS of two strings for $\delta=H$}. Given two strings $s_1$ and $s_2$, each of length $n$, find a longest substring (of either $s_1$ or $s_2$) that occurs in both $s_1$ and $s_2$ with at most $k$ mismatches. 

In 2011, Babenko and Starikovskaya~\cite{Babenko2011ComputingMismatch} solve this problem for $k=1$ in $\bigo (n^2)$-time and $\bigo (n)$-space complexity. This result for $k=1$ was later improved by Flouri \textit{et al.} in~\cite{Flouri2015LongestMismatches}, where they propose an $\bigo(n \log n)$-time and $\bigo(n)$-space approach. 

Leimeister and Morgenstern, in 2014, introduced this problem for $k>1$ and proposed a greedy heuristic algorithm to solve it~\cite{Leimeister2014Kmacs:Comparison}. Since then, several researchers have worked on this problem and presented improved solutions. In particular, Flouri \textit{et al.} in 2015~\cite{Flouri2015LongestMismatches} presented a simple and elegant $\bigo(n^2)$-time algorithm that uses constant additional space. Grabowski~\cite{Grabowski2015AProblem} presented two output-dependent algorithms, with $\bigo (n((k+1)(l_o+1))^k)$ and $\bigo(n^2k/l_k)$ time complexities, where $l_o$ is the length of the ELCS of $s_1$ and $s_2$ and $l_k$ is the length of the ALCS of $s_1$ and $s_2$. Abboud \textit{et al.} in~\cite{Abboud2015MoreDesign} described a $k^{1.5}n^2/2^{\Omega(\sqrt{(\log n)/k})}$-time randomized solution to this problem. In 2016, Thankachan \textit{et al.}~\cite{Thankachan2016AProblem} presented an $\bigo (n \log^k n)$-time and $\bigo (n)$-space solution. Then in 2018, Charalampopoulos \textit{et al.} proved an $\bigo (n)$-time and space solution where the length of $ALCS$ is $\Omega(\log^{2k+2} n)$~\cite{Charalampopoulos2018Linear-timeMismatches}. In 2019, Kociumaka \textit{et al.} showed that assuming the Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (SETH)~\cite{Impagliazzo2001OnK-SAT}\cite{Impagliazzo2001WhichComplexity}, no strongly subquadratic-time solution for this problem exists for $k \in \Omega(\log n)$~\cite{Kociumaka2019LongestMismatches}. 
Finally in 2021, Charalampopoulos \textit{et al.}~\cite{Charalampopoulos2021FasterSubstring} showed that this problem can be solved in $\bigo (n \log^{k-1/2} n)$-time and $\bigo (n)$-space complexity.\newline\textbf{ALCS of two strings for $\delta=\{L,E\}$}. Given two strings $s_1$ and $s_2$, each of length $n$, find a longest substring (of either $s_1$ or $s_2$) that occurs in both $s_1$ and $s_2$ with at most $k$ edit operations.

In 2015 Abboud \textit{et al.} described a $k^{1.5}n^2/2^{\Omega(\sqrt{(\log n)/k})}$-time randomized solution~\cite{Abboud2015MoreDesign} for ALCS of two strings for $\delta=\{L,E\}$. In 2018, Thankachan \textit{et al.} proposed an $\bigo (n \log^k n)$-time and $\bigo (n)$-space solution to this problem~\cite{thankachan2018algorithmic}.

\section{Results on ALCS Problems on Multiple Strings}\label{sec:main}
\subsection{NP-Hardness of \texorpdfstring{$k$-LCS and $k$-$t$ LCS}{k-LCS and k-t LCS}}\label{ssec:onComplexOfProb1n3}

The $k$-LCS problem for $\delta={H}$ (defined in Section~\ref{sec:DefforApproxLCS}) is called the \textit{Longest Common Approximate Substring} problem in~\cite{smith2004common}, and has been proved to be NP-hard by reducing the \textit{Vertex Cover} problem to it. It has also been shown that this problem cannot be approximated in polynomial time with a performance ratio better than $2-\varepsilon$, for any $\varepsilon > 0$, unless $P=NP$ \cite[Theorems 4.12 \& 4.13]{smith2004common}. Hence, we have the following:

\begin{theorem}\label{theo:npklcs}
    The $k$-LCS problem is NP-hard for $\delta={H}$ .
\end{theorem}

    \textbf{(Partial Vertex Cover)} Given a graph $G=(V,E)$, positive integers $k, t$, is there a vertex subset $C \subseteq V$, $|C| \leq k$, such that $C$ covers at least $t$ edges?

It is shown that \textit{Partial Vertex Cover} is NP-hard~\cite{caskurlu2014partial,gandhi2004approximation,halperin2002improved}. We can make a reduction from \textit{Partial Vertex Cover} to $k$-$t$ LCS. We construct an instance of $k$-$t$ LCS with $|E|$ strings corresponding to edges in $G$. $k$-approximate occurrence of a $k$-$t$ LCS in at least $t$ strings of set $S$ corresponds to a \textit{Partial Vertex Cover}, covering at least $t$ edges. String gadget construction for this reduction remains similar to~\cite{smith2004common}.

\begin{theorem}
    The $k$-$t$ LCS problem is NP-hard for $\delta={H}$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Polynomial Time Solution for Restricted ALCS 
}\label{sec:poly}
In this section, we solve the four restricted variations of the ALCS problem identified by prefix \textit{Rk} in Section~\ref{sec:DefforApproxLCS}.
For this, we use an auxiliary table called \textit{lengthStat} (Definition~\ref{def:ls}).

For strings $s_1$ and $s_2$, and $k \in \mathbb{N}$, $LCP^{H,k}_{(s_1,s_2)}[i',j']$ for $1\leq i' \leq |s_1|$ and $1\leq j' \leq |s_2|$, is the length of the longest common prefix with at most $k$ mismatches, of suffixes $s_1[i'..|s_1|]$ and $s_2[j'..|s_2|]$. An example of $LCP^{H,k}_{(s_1,s_2)}$ is shown in Table~\ref{fig:lcpkexample}.

