\begin{theorem}\label{theo:rklcshard}
    Suppose there is a $\varepsilon > 0$ such that Rk-LCS for any $m$ can be solved in $\bigo(N^{2-\varepsilon})$ time on binary strings for $k=\Omega(\log \ell)$. Then SETH is false.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    By Lemma~\ref{lem:instance} and $q=1$, we construct an instance of the $OV$ problem with $N_v$ vectors of dimension $d$. This instance is equivalent to an instance of the $Rk$-$LCS$ problem, where $k=2md$ and the length of each string is $\ell=(8m+5)(2N_v+1)d$. Assuming that the $Rk$-$LCS$ problem for any arbitrary $m$ can be solved in $\bigo(N^{2-\varepsilon})$ time for $k=\Omega(\log \ell)$, we can solve the constructed instance of the $OV$ problem in $\bigo(N^{2-\varepsilon}_vd^{O(1)})$ time for $d=c \log N_v$. This contradicts SETH. \QEDB
\end{proof}

\section{Conclusion and Open Problems}
In this paper, we identify several problems related to approximate LCS (ALCS) of multiple strings; that is, for a set of strings $\s{S}=\{s_1,s_2,\ldots,s_m\}$, where $m \geq 2$. We show that for given $k$, the $k$-LCS and $k$-$t$ LCS problems are NP-hard, even under Hamming distance. We propose efficient solutions to the restricted variations of the ALCS problem on multiple strings. In particular, we show that these variations can be computed in $\bigo(N^2)$ and $\bigo(k\ell N^2)$ under Hamming and edit distances, respectively, where $n$ is the total length of all strings and $\ell$ is the length of each string in set \s{S}. We also show that for $\delta=\{H,L,E\}$ using the $k$-errata trie data structure, the $Rk$-LCS and other restricted ALCS problems can be computed in $\bigo(mN\log^k \ell)$-time and $\bigo(\binom{m}{t} mN\log^k \ell)$-time, respectively.
Theoretically, we note that for the $Rk$-LCS problem, the polylog-time solution outperforms the polynomial-time solution for $k << \ell$. Finally, we demonstrate the SETH-hardness of $Rk$-LCS problem. As part of future work, it would be of interest to implement the polylog-time approaches and compare their performance with the quadratic-time solutions. Moreover, it would be valuable to propose sub-quadratic algorithms for these problems on compressed strings, either within the \texttt{PILLAR} model or in a quantum-computing framework.

