\begin{abstract}
%\fix{NEED TO REWRITE}
% This research unveils a new fabrication method where two-dimensional flat sheets transform into intricate three-dimensional structures autonomously, through an interplay of contracting thermoplastics and strategic kirigami cuts. Inspired by natural morphogenesis, where structures form and grow without external manipulation, our methodology leverages the inherent thermal responsiveness of Shrinky Dinks. Integrated with 3D-printed kirigami patterns, these composites self-transform upon the application of heat, morphing into pre-designed forms such as bowls, pyramids, and specialized objects like computer mouse covers. By fine-tuning initial parameters such as the kirigami cuts and geometrical settings, the transformation process self-directs, creating complex shapes with precision and without the need for ongoing external inputs. Utilizing common tools like household ovens and 3D printers, this method provides a simple, scalable approach to fabricating self-assembling structures, enhancing the feasibility of deployable materials across various applications and promoting innovation in adaptive design and engineering.
%
We present a simple, accessible method for autonomously transforming flat plastic sheets into intricate three-dimensional structures using only uniform heating and common tools such as household ovens and scissors. Our approach combines heat-shrinkable thermoplastics with Kirigami patterns tailored to the target 3D shape, creating bilayer composites that morph into a wide range of complex structures, e.g., bowls, pyramids, and even custom ergonomic surfaces like mouse covers. Critically, the transformation is driven by a low-information stimulus (uniform heat) yet produces highly intricate shapes through programmed geometric design. The morphing behavior, confirmed by finite element simulations, arises from strain mismatch between the contracting thermoplastic layer and the constraining Kirigami layer. By decoupling material composition from mechanical response, this method avoids detailed process control and enables a broad class of self-morphing structures, offering a versatile platform for adaptive design and scalable manufacturing.
\end{abstract}

