We perform a study to understand the variation of the final 3D shape based on the fabrication parameters such as \fix{}. To do so, we use the hemispherical bowl shape as the basis for comparison. We keep parameters such as outer radius and thickness same of the circular foundation of the kirigami design Fig.~\ref{fig:parametricvariation}A. However, to understand the effect of the change in kirigami design on the final 3D shape, we vary the ratio of the inner radius to the outer radius. To do so, we employ the same kirigami design above with the 8 equally spaced petals, and vary the radius ratio ($\gamma$). In doing so, we vary the percentage of material removed ($\alpha$) as follows:
\begin{equation}
    \alpha = 0.5 \times (1-\gamma^2) 
\end{equation}

We thus, understand the effect of variation in $\gamma$ on the final height of the structure. We see that the normalized height ($H/2R$) decreases as you increase $\gamma$ from 0.05 to 1. This is because if the amount of kirigami layer is lesser, the Shrinky Dink layer is more free to deform and the composite buckles more. Note that, we avoid radius ratios lesser than 0.05 due to physical geometric limitations. Fig. \ref{fig:parametricvariation}B shows this trend in both the simulations and the experiments. Experimentally, in \ref{fig:parametricvariation}C, we can see the height increasing with increase in $\alpha$ both in the experiments and the simulations.
