% Semantic map models build a graph-like conceptual space constrained by local connectivity hypothesis, where nodes are meanings or functions, and edges show the degree of association between nodes. This method is widely used in the area of typological linguistics to compare the interrelated meanings among different cross-lingual forms. Usually experts manually create the space in a bottom-to-up manner but it is inefficient to extend to the larger-scale data. They lack a visualization tool to display and evaluate the space neither. To address these limitations, this demonstration paper develops an interative tool XISM to visualize and evaluate the semantic map models (SMMs), based on our previous algorithm. Our tool first builds SMMs from the user data in a top-down algorithm, displays candidate results, and evaluate them by several metrics. Users later can edit the edges to refine the map by their own considerations. The human-in-the-loop manner combines the advantages of data-driven machine efficiency and knowledge-driven capacity, which is a benefit for typologist and computational linguists. The system~\footnote{\url{https://770103knev48.vicp.fun/}} with a demonstration video~\footnote{~\url{https://youtu.be/S-wsVDF2HSI?si=1OrcF41tRznaifhZ}} have been released.

Semantic map models represent meanings or functions as nodes in a graph constrained by the local connectivity hypothesis, with edges indicating their associations. Widely used in typological linguistics, these models compare interrelated meanings across languages. Traditionally built manually in a bottom-up manner, they are inefficient for large datasets and lack visualization and evaluation tools.
%
This paper introduces XISM, an interactive tool based on our prior algorithm, which constructs semantic maps from user data via a top-down approach, displays candidate maps, and evaluates them using multiple metrics. Users can refine maps by editing edges, combining data-driven efficiency with expert knowledge. This human-in-the-loop design benefits both typologists and computational linguists.
%
The system\footnote{\url{https://770103knev48.vicp.fun/}} and a demonstration video\footnote{\url{https://youtu.be/S-wsVDF2HSI?si=1OrcF41tRznaifhZ}} are publicly available.



