\subsection{Codestream features}

The codestream contains one image, which has specific dimensions (up to $2^{30} \times 2^{30}$) and components: the main image data has either one (grayscale) or three (color) components, and there can be up to 4096 additional components, known as `extra channels'.
The image dimensions define the size (in pixels) of a rectangular canvas.
An image consists of one or more frames that are rendered on this canvas. Frames can represent animation frames, layers of a composite still image, or individual pages of a multi-page image. Each frame contains pixel data (samples for each component) that is encoded either using VarDCT mode or Modular mode.
The data is subdivided into groups, which can be coded independently, allowing parallelization and region-of-interest decoding.
% thor: not yet clear how frames are to be rendered. Maybe it should be said here that frames are supposed to be shown/rendered at particular time stamps, forming an animation. Other ways to interpret "frames" would be to compose an overall image from sub-images, thus some additional clarity is required.

\subsubsection{Frames}

A JPEG~XL codestream contains one or more frames. In the case of animation, these frames have a duration and can be looped (infinitely or a specific number of times).
Zero-duration frames are possible and represent different layers of the image.
Maximum-duration frames indicate `page breaks' in a multi-page image.
% thor: repeat, please clear up with the above.

Frames have a blend mode (Replace, Add, Alpha-blend, Multiply, etc.) and they can use any previous frame as a base.
They can be smaller than the image canvas, in which case the pixels outside the crop are copied from the base frame.
Frames can be positioned at an arbitrary offset from the image canvas. This offset can also be negative and frames can also be larger than the image canvas, in which case parts of the frame will be invisible and only the intersection with the image canvas will be shown.
It is also possible to have invisible `ReferenceOnly' frames that are not rendered, but that can still be used as a base for frame blending or as a source for Patches.
% thor: it might be helpful to introduce "canvas" as a concept, same as "frame" or "codestream". This is a bit too rough.
% jon: elaborated the first paragraph a bit.

By default, decoders will blend and coalesce frames, producing only a single output frame when there are subsequent zero-duration frames.
As a result, all output frames are of the same size (the size of the image canvas) and have either no duration (in case of a still image) or a non-zero duration (in case of animation).
This causes layered images to be shown in viewing applications as a single merged image, cropped to the image canvas, while authoring tools can still decode the individual layers.
It also simplifies the implementation of animation viewers since viewers do not need to implement frame blending at the application level.

