\begin{abstract}
The proliferation of propaganda on mobile platforms raises critical concerns around detection accuracy and user privacy. To address this, we propose \textbf{TRIDENT}—a \textit{three-tier propaganda detection model implementing transformers, adversarial learning, and differential privacy} which integrates syntactic obfuscation and label perturbation to mitigate privacy leakage while maintaining propaganda detection accuracy. TRIDENT leverages multilingual back-translation to introduce semantic variance, character-level noise, and entity obfuscation for differential privacy enforcement, and combines these techniques into a unified defense mechanism. Using a binary propaganda classification dataset, baseline transformer models (BERT, GPT-2) we achieved F1 scores of $0.89$ and $0.90$. Applying TRIDENT's third-tier defense yields a reduced but effective cumulative F1 of $0.83$, demonstrating strong privacy protection across mobile ML deployments with minimal degradation.
\end{abstract}

