 % data from different modalities vary significantly in data format and statistical properties, making it ineffective to process them with models that lack modality-specific adaptations.

Most learning-based lossless compressors are designed for a single modality, requiring separate models for multi-modal data and lacking flexibility. However, different modalities vary significantly in format and statistical properties, making it ineffective to use compressors that lack modality-specific adaptations. 
While multi-modal large language models (MLLMs) offer a potential solution for modality-unified compression, their excessive complexity hinders practical deployment. To address these challenges, we focus on the two most common modalities, image and text, and propose \textbf{\emph{DualComp}}, \textbf{the first unified and lightweight learning-based dual-modality lossless compressor}. Built on a lightweight backbone, \emph{DualComp} incorporates three key structural enhancements to handle modality heterogeneity: modality-unified tokenization, modality-switching contextual learning, and modality-routing mixture-of-experts. A reparameterization training strategy is also used to boost compression performance. DualComp integrates both modality-specific and shared parameters for efficient parameter utilization, enabling near real-time inference ($200$KB/s) on desktop CPUs. With much fewer parameters, DualComp achieves compression performance on par with the SOTA LLM-based methods for both text and image datasets. Its simplified single-modality variant surpasses the previous best image compressor on the Kodak dataset by about $9\%$ using just 1.2\% of the model size. 
% With no more than 130M parameters, DualComp achieves compression performance on par with the SOTA LLM-based methods for both text and image. Its simplified single-modality variant also surpasses the previous best image compressor on the Kodak dataset by about $9\%$ using just 1.2\% of the model size. Our code will be released upon acceptance.

% Our DualComp integrates both modality-specific and shared parameters and boasts highly efficient parameter utilization, providing near real-time ($>40KB/s$) inference on CPU of desktop devices. With only tens of millions of parameters, it achieves compression performance comparable to current SOTA methods for both image and text. Additionally, our simplified single-modality variant outperforms the previous best method by approximately $9\%$ on image compression using only $1/10$ of the previous best method's parameters. Our code will be released upon acceptance.






% Large language models (LLMs) have exhibited superior in-context learning capabilities, achieving great success across various tasks. LLMs can seamlessly function as probabilistic models, integrating with an entropy encoder for lossless data compression. However, due to their high computational complexity, their potential as lossless data compressors has been largely overlooked. In this paper, we propose a unified and lightweight framework for dual-modal lossless data compression. First, we treat images and text as tokens, and propose a novel outlier-aware tokenizer that encode frequent tokens while allowing outliers to bypass the prediction. Second, we utilize the low-complexity RWKV design and propose a novel high-rank reparameterization strategy based on RWKV that enhances the learning capability during training without increasing complexity during inference. It is worthy noting that our proposed design can be applied to multimodal data, using a unified framework. Experimental results have validated the effectiveness of our method. For text compression, our method achieves 48\% bit saving compared to gzip compressor and offers compression performance comparable to other learned compressors, with a $50\times$ reduction in model parameters. {\color{red}For image compression, our method can outperform PNG and XXX.}
