Theorem \ref{thm:bias} quantifies the maximum bias and the ``expected bias magnitude'' of the Subspace Predictor. % $S$, which provide proxy measurements of the expected degradation in performance of subspace-based DDPC methods as the span of the Hankel matrix for DeePC \cite{coulson2019data} (or the L matrix for $\gamma$-DDPC \cite{breschi2022unified}) does not match the span of the true LTI system after the feedback controller $f(\cdot)$ has been removed. Thus, regardless of how much data is used, the $u_f$ and $y_f$ produced by subspace-based DDPC methods will not be consistent with the true system dynamics.
% We note that the bias of the subspace prediction $\widehat{S} v$, given in Theorem \ref{thm:bias}, is different than the bias of the parameters of $\widehat{s}$, which are derived in \cite{dinkla2023bias}.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:bias} 
    $\\$ %gives a new line
    Consider an LTI system, i.e., \eqref{eqn:sysDef}, %in which training data has been gathered using a feedback controller $f(\cdot)$,

    \noindent a) If training data is collected in open loop,
    $ b_{S}(v) = 0$.


    \noindent b) If the training data is gathered using feedback, 
    \begin{align*} 
        \begin{aligned} %\max_{v,g}  b_{S}(v) \\
        &\underset{v,g}{\text{max}} \;\; \left\lVert b_{S}(v) \right\lVert \\
        &\text{s.t.} \;\ v = V g \text{, } \|g\| = 1  
        \end{aligned} 
        \quad = \sigma_\text{max} \left( \beta_V \right) \neq 0,
    \end{align*}
    % \begin{align*}
    %     \left\lVert \max_{v} b(\frac{v}{\left\Vert v\right\Vert}) \right\lVert = \sigma_\text{max} \left( \beta_V \right) \neq 0,
    % \end{align*}
    where $\beta_V$ is the Subspace Empirical Bias.
    Furthermore, defining $p(V)$ as the distribution from which $V$ was drawn,
    \begin{align*}
        \!\mathbb{E}_{v \sim p(V)} \!\! \left[ \left\Vert b_{S}(v)\right\Vert^2\right] \!&=\! \left\Vert H_\epsilon \! \begin{bmatrix}
            0 & \mathbb{E}\big[\Epsilon_f U_f^T\big]
        \end{bmatrix} \mathbb{E}\left[ V V^T \right]^{-1/2}\right\Vert^2_F \!.
    \end{align*}
\end{theorem}

Theorem \ref{thm:bias} states that the Subspace Predictor is unbiased when the training data is collected in open loop, but not, in general, when the data is collected in closed loop.
Specifically, the first part of Theorem \ref{thm:bias}b) provides a worst-case bias magnitude %for a norm-constrained $g$ vector 
% This bound is relevant as $\|g\|$ is correlated to $||v||$. %---thus, the maximum bias of the subspace predictor is proportional to the magnitude of the initial condition and the control input.
% This worst-case bound 
which is important for optimization-based control because the optimizer may ``seek out'' the behavior described by the bias. 
The second part of Theorem \ref{thm:bias}b) gives the ``expected bias magnitude''---the Subspace Bias if $v$ is drawn randomly from the same distribution as the training data $V$. While $v$ will not be drawn from the same distribution as $V$ in DDPC applications, the expected bias magnitude provides a ballpark-range for the Subspace Bias. % if $v$ is in some way similar to the columns of $V$.

