In summary, our contributions are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
  \item We propose TalkFashion, an intelligent virtual try-on assistant that allows both full outfit changes and local detail adjustments through simple user text instructions.
  \item We introduce the text-image matching mechanism in the function invocation process, enabling the unification of both image-based and text-based virtual try-on models using pure text instructions for full outfit change.
  % \item We enhance existing try-on networks by introducing multimodal models to extract more specific semantic information from garment images, reducing the cost of labeling garment attributes for realistic detail generation.
  \item We propose an instruction-based local repainting model that enables fully automated local editings with text instructions. With the help of multi-modal models, it eliminates the need for users to manually provide masks and achieves more natural and accurate editing results.
\end{itemize}


\section{Related Work}

\subsection{Virtual Try-on}

Current virtual try-on primarily relies on Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)\cite{gan} or Latent Diffusion Models (LDMs) \cite{stablediffusion} for image synthesis. In GAN-based VTON methods \cite{viton, acgpn, hr-viton, gp-vton}, the main approach involves warping the clothing image and then aligning it with the human's image. However, these methods often struggle with alignment issues caused by explicit flow estimation or distortions and lack generalization to arbitrary person images, such as those with complex backgrounds or intricate poses. With the rapid development of diffusion models \cite{adapter, dragondiffusion, diffeditor}, diffusion model-based virtual try-on methods \cite{stableviton, ootdiffusion, idmvton} have begun to shine. They consider virtual try-on as an exemplar-based image inpainting problem, fine-tuning the inpainting diffusion models on a virtual try-on dataset to generate high-quality virtual try-on images. This approach allows for controlling the generation of clothing through outline sketches, line drawings, or text descriptions, without requiring users to upload specific images of the desired garments. However, these methods still do not fully exploit the semantic information of clothing images, resulting in incomplete preservation of garment details. They also lack the flexibility to perform editings in specific regions, limiting their adaptability to more nuanced user requirements.

% Current virtual try-on primarily relies on Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) \cite{gan} or Latent Diffusion Models (LDMs) \cite{stablediffusion} for image synthesis. In GAN-based VTON methods \cite{viton, cp-viton, acgpn, pf-afn, hr-viton, gp-vton}, the main approach involves warping the clothing image and then aligning it with the human's image. CP-VTON \cite{cp-viton} explicitly delineates the try-on into warping and generation stages. HR-VITON \cite{hr-viton} proposes a try-on condition generator as a unified module for both the warping and segmentation generation stages. GP-VTON \cite{gp-vton} uses local flows to warp garment parts individually and assembles the locally warped results via global garment parsing. However, these methods often struggle with alignment issues caused by explicit flow estimation or distortions and lack generalization to arbitrary person images, such as those with complex backgrounds or intricate poses. With the rapid development of diffusion models, diffusion model-based virtual try-on methods \cite{tryondiffusion, ladi-vton, stableviton, ootdiffusion, idmvton} have begun to shine. They consider virtual try-on as an exemplar-based image inpainting problem, fine-tuning the inpainting diffusion models on a virtual try-on dataset to generate high-quality virtual try-on images. StableVITON \cite{stableviton} introduces zero cross-attention blocks that not only preserve clothing details by learning semantic correspondence but also generate high-fidelity images by leveraging the inherent knowledge of the pre-trained model in the generation process. OOTDiffusion \cite{ootdiffusion} fine-tunes a pre-trained outfitting UNet to learn garment details and align them with the target human body through outfitting fusion. IDM-VTON \cite{idmvton} designs sophisticated attention modules to condition garment images to the diffusion model and provides detailed textual prompts for both garment and person images to enhance the authenticity of the generated visuals. Some work \cite{unihuman, mgd, ti-mgd} utilizes textual and visual prompts to control the image generation process in text-to-image diffusion models. This approach allows for controlling the generation of clothing through outline sketches, line drawings, or text descriptions, without requiring users to upload specific images of the desired garments. However, these methods still do not fully exploit the semantic information of clothing images, resulting in incomplete preservation of garment details. They also lack the flexibility to perform editings in specific regions, limiting their adaptability to more nuanced user requirements.


